      "description": "The Boren Awards fund intensive overseas language study for U.S. undergraduate students (Boren Scholarships) and research or language study for U.S. graduate students (Boren Fellowships). They pay a fixed grant of $8,000, $12,500 or $25,000 depending on the duration of overseas study, and the programme states plainly that the award \"is not all-inclusive\" and is designed to cover only the majority of an awardee's overseas expenses. In exchange, recipients commit to at least one year of federal service in support of U.S. national security after graduation.",
      "program_type": "Government scholarship",
      "best_fit": "U.S. citizens aged 18 or over who are matriculated at an accredited U.S. undergraduate or graduate degree programme, can commit 8-52 weeks to overseas study of a Boren-preferred language in a Boren-preferred region, and intend to pursue a federal national-security career.",
      "funding_amount": null,
      "funding_currency": null,
      "funding_period": null,
      "eligibility_summary": "U.S. citizens aged 18 or older, matriculated at an accredited U.S. undergraduate or graduate programme, proposing a continuous programme of at least 12 weeks (8 weeks for undergraduate STEM) of classroom language study in a country outside Western Europe, Canada, Australia and New Zealand, and of which they are not a citizen.",
      "eligibility": [
        "U.S. citizens at the time of the national application deadline",
        "A high school graduate, or have earned a GED",
        "At least 18 years of age or older by the time the application is submitted",
        "Planning an overseas program in an overseas destination outside of Western Europe, Canada, Australia, or New Zealand",
        "Planning to study in an overseas destination of which they are not a citizen",
        "Matriculated for the duration of their Boren Awards-funded program in an undergraduate or graduate degree program located within the United States and accredited by a body recognized by the U.S. Department of Education",
        "For ROTC cadets, midshipmen and members of the military Reserves or National Guard: in inactive, non-drilling status for the duration of their Boren Awards-funded program",
        "Boren Scholarships applicants must be matriculated in an associate's or bachelor's degree program at the time of application and must remain matriculated in an undergraduate degree program; they may not graduate until the scholarship is complete",
        "Boren Fellowships applicants may be matriculated in or applying to a graduate degree program, but Boren Fellows must be matriculated in a graduate degree program by the start date of the fellowship and may not graduate until the fellowship is complete",
        "Eligible graduate degree programs include master's (MA, MS, MBA, LLM and others), juris doctor (JD), or doctoral (PhD, MD and others) programs; non-degree certificate programs are not eligible by themselves"
      ],
      "coverage": [
        "Approved program tuition and fees",
        "Home institution matriculation fees, up to $2,500/semester ($5,000 overall cap)",
        "Room and board (housing and food)",
        "Books and supplies",
        "Visa and overseas visa-related costs",
        "Fly America Act compliant round-trip airfare at the lowest cost unrestricted class (basic economy)",
        "Checked luggage (up to 2 bags)",
        "Overseas health insurance",
        "Local transportation",
        "Cybersecurity costs including a burner laptop and/or phone, or VPN (up to $500)",
        "Select research-related expenses (Boren Fellows only)",
        "One flight change fee related to approved travel and/or an approved change to program dates",
        "Regional Flagship Language Initiative awards add the approved summer domestic cost on top of the overseas tier amount"
      ],
      "benefits": [
        "NSEP Service Team support, including resume reviews, career fairs, a mentorship program and advocacy on behalf of award recipients to human resources and hiring officials at U.S. government agencies",
        "Access to NSEPnet, which features job listings and positions for federal government agencies exclusive to awardees",
        "Non-competitive eligibility: until they fulfill the federal service requirement in full, Boren award recipients are eligible for non-competitive appointments to many federal positions",
        "An annual Federal Employment Seminar in Washington, DC with training on the federal hiring process and networking with senior officials from federal agencies and contractors",
        "Access to the NSEP Alumni Association"
      ],
      "requirements": [
        "Two letters of recommendation for Boren Scholarships (a third letter is optional); three letters of recommendation for Boren Fellowships, with at least one from a research advisor at the U.S. institution if a research component is proposed",
        "Recommenders may be academic or professional contacts who know the applicant well; they may not be family or friends",
        "Essay 1 - National Security, maximum 800 words, single-spaced",
        "Essay 2 - Motivation and Public Service Careers, maximum 800 words for Boren Scholarships and 1000 words for Boren Fellowships, single-spaced",
        "Study Plan Summary: maximum 250 words for each segment of the proposed overseas program, plus descriptions of alternate overseas destination programs",
        "Weekly contact hours of formal language acquisition - the Boren Awards requires overseas programs contain at least 15-20 weekly language hours",
        "An alternate study abroad program is required for applicants choosing an overseas destination marked with an asterisk (blue on the map) or for the languages Arabic, Mandarin or Russian; it is not required for Mandarin in Taiwan, Arabic in Morocco, or Language Flagship capstone applicants",
        "Employment History: up to three jobs, each described in 50 to 100 words",
        "Activities: up to three extracurricular activities, each described in 50 to 100 words",
        "Language Self-Assessment in the online application, plus up to four additional languages studied or spoken",
        "Up to three non-U.S. countries where you have lived, traveled or studied; up to five relevant honours and awards; optional list of up to five other scholarships applied for",
        "Applicants must agree to the policies in the Submission Information section in order to actually submit",
        "Boren Scholars whose airfare is funded by Boren must comply with the Fly America Act and send a photocopy of the passenger receipt page(s) to their airline to show compliance",
        "All Boren-funded programs must include language study within a classroom as a core element for the duration of the grant"
      ],
      "english_requirement": null,
      "application_method": [
        "Visit the Apply Now page and select the overseas location and language you want to study",
        "Reach out to your Boren Awards campus representative at your institution for advice (you may still apply if your institution does not have one)",
        "Watch the introductory webinar and review the official selection criteria",
        "Choose a programme: apply for a Regional Flagship Language Initiative or select/design your own study programme",
        "Complete and review the essays with your recommenders",
        "Submit the application through the online application system by the national deadline"
      ],
      "selection_notes": "Applications are evaluated by IIE-organized regional merit review panels made up of faculty members and administrators from colleges and universities across the United States, representing a wide array of fields of study. The panels make recommendations on each submitted, eligible application, with final decision-making authority held by the program's federal sponsor, DLNSEO. There is no semi-finalist or interview phase, so everything an applicant would want the selection committee to know must be stated in the essays and other application materials. Selection preference is given to veterans when other factors are equivalent.",
      "notes": "Non-deferrable in practice: recipients must remain matriculated at their U.S. institution for the whole funded period and may not graduate until the award is complete, and overseas programmes must be approved by a Boren Program Advisor before travel. Not renewable as published - the award is a fixed one-time grant tier set by overseas duration, and requests to increase awards above the published maximums are contingent upon availability of funds. No return-home obligation is published; instead recipients must complete at least one year of federal service in support of U.S. national security after graduation, with preference given to a longer-term commitment. Internships conducted during a Boren-funded period may not be remunerated nor conducted with the U.S. or a foreign government, and Boren Fellows may not work as journalists while abroad, even freelance or unpaid. Funding for programme expenses incurred after May 31, 2028 is contingent upon availability and cannot be guaranteed at the time of award.",
      "image_url": "https://www.borenawards.org/themes/custom/borenawards/logo.svg",
      "image_source_url": "https://www.borenawards.org/",
      "image_source_type": "official_provider",
      "image_verified_at": "2026-10-01",
      "image_alt_text": "Boren Awards",
      "detail": {
        "official_details": {
          "description": "The Boren Awards are a component of the National Security Education Program (NSEP) that funds the intensive study of language abroad by U.S. undergraduate and graduate students who commit to federal service. Boren Scholarships support undergraduates and Boren Fellowships support graduate students; both require a continuous period of overseas study with at least 15-20 weekly language contact hours. The award is a fixed grant tier determined by duration of overseas study ($8,000 / $12,500 / $25,000) and is explicitly not all-inclusive: the programme states that funding \"is designed to cover the majority of an awardee's overseas expenses\" and lists a closed set of eligible and ineligible costs.",
          "purpose": [
            "The National Security Education Program (NSEP) awards Boren Scholarships and Fellowships to U.S. undergraduate and graduate students committed to long-term overseas study and to federal service",
            "The Boren Awards fund the intensive study of language abroad by U.S. undergraduate or graduate students",
            "The Boren Awards promote long-term linguistic immersion and regional experience; proposed programs of two or more semesters are strongly encouraged and preference is given to programs of at least 25 weeks",
            "The Boren Awards invest in linguistic and cultural knowledge for aspiring federal government employees",
            "One objective of the Boren Scholarship is to expand international educational opportunities for students who have not studied abroad and who may require financial assistance to do so",
            "The Boren Awards award scholarships and fellowships that have launched alumni careers into the federal workforce"
          ],
          "sdg_requirement": false,
          "sdg_requirement_details": [],
          "degree_levels": [
            "Boren Scholarships: associate's or bachelor's degree programme (undergraduate)",
            "Boren Fellowships: master's (MA, MS, MBA, LLM and others), juris doctor (JD), or doctoral (PhD, MD and others)"
          ],
          "study_type": [
            "Overseas language immersion study",
            "Overseas research tied to a graduate thesis or dissertation (Boren Fellows)",
            "Academic internship abroad (Boren Fellows only; unpaid, not with a U.S. or foreign government)",
            "Regional Flagship Language Initiative: credit-bearing language courses in a domestic summer term and an overseas fall term, with the option to continue overseas into the spring"
          ],
          "degree_award": "Not a degree award. The Boren Awards fund overseas study or research undertaken alongside a U.S. degree programme; recipients must remain matriculated in a U.S. degree programme and may not graduate until the award is complete.",
          "duration": "8 to 52 weeks of overseas study. Minimum 12 weeks for most applicants; a special exception allows undergraduate STEM majors to propose summer programmes of 8 weeks or longer. Preference is given to 25 weeks or more. 2027-cycle funding may be applied to programmes starting as early as June 2027 or as late as March 1, 2028.",
          "application_cycle": "Annual. The 2027-2028 cycle covers both Boren Scholarships and Boren Fellowships.",
          "deadline_central": "2027-01-20",
          "deadline_central_display": "Boren Fellowships: January 20, 2027, 5pm Eastern time. Boren Scholarships: January 27, 2027, 5pm Eastern time.",
          "deadline_institutional": "A Boren Awards campus representative may set an earlier campus deadline to provide support and feedback; applicants are asked to respect it. Applicants not supported by a campus representative may still apply without penalty.",
          "deadline_note": "Two national deadlines exist for the 2027-2028 cycle, one per award type. The primary deadline_date on this record is the earlier Boren Fellowship deadline (2027-01-20); Boren Scholarship applicants have until 2027-01-27.",
          "status": "OPEN",
          "funding_type": "Partial Funding",
          "funding_label": "Boren Scholarships: $8,000 for 8-11 weeks (summer programme, STEM majors only); $12,500 for 12-24 weeks; $25,000 for 25-52 weeks. Boren Fellowships: $12,500 for 12-24 weeks; $25,000 for 25-52 weeks. Regional Flagship Language Initiative: approved summer domestic cost PLUS $12,500 (12-24 weeks overseas) or $25,000 (25-52 weeks overseas).",
          "funding_amount": null,
          "funding_currency": null,
          "funding_period": null,
          "tuition_coverage": "Approved program tuition and fees are eligible, plus home institution matriculation fees up to $2,500 per semester ($5,000 overall cap). This is reimbursement against a closed list of eligible costs, not a tuition waiver at either institution.",
          "living_cost_coverage": "Room and board (housing and food) and books and supplies are eligible expenses, but the award is capped and the programme states the award \"is not all-inclusive\".",
          "travel_coverage": "Fly America Act compliant round-trip airfare at the lowest cost unrestricted class (basic economy). The page states explicitly that the Open Skies Agreement does not apply to Boren funding. Visa and visa-related costs, checked luggage (up to 2 bags), local transportation and one flight-change fee related to approved travel or approved date changes are also eligible.",
          "fully_funded": false,
          "tuition_rule": "Nobody waives tuition. The applicant must remain matriculated at an accredited U.S. institution for the whole funded period, and overseas program tuition and fees plus home-institution matriculation fees (capped) are reimbursable eligible costs against the fixed grant.",
          "home_tuition_rule": "Home-institution obligations continue: Boren Scholars and Fellows must stay matriculated at their U.S. institution and may not graduate until the scholarship or fellowship is complete. Home matriculation fees are eligible up to $2,500 per semester, $5,000 overall.",
          "host_institution_fee": "Home institution matriculation fees, up to $2,500 per semester with a $5,000 overall cap, are an eligible Boren expense.",
          "tax_status": null,
          "covered_expenses": [
