      "english_requirement": null,
      "application_method": [
        "Create a Research.gov account if you do not already have one.",
        "Access the GRFP Application Module, which usually opens in July or August.",
        "Enter accurate personal information.",
        "Select your eligibility level (Level 1 or Level 2).",
        "Enter education, work and other experience, including all degree-granting institutions attended.",
        "Upload official undergraduate transcripts in PDF format, and graduate transcripts where applicable.",
        "Select the Major Field of Study and subfield that is most closely aligned with the proposed graduate program and research plan; this fixes your deadline and reviewer panel.",
        "Enter the names, organizational affiliations and email addresses of three reference writers, ranked in order of preference, and submit the request; reference writers are notified by email with instructions.",
        "Draft and upload the Personal, Relevant Background, and Future Goals Statement (three pages maximum, PDF only).",
        "Draft and upload the Graduate Research Plan Statement (two pages maximum, PDF only).",
        "Receive at least two reference letters by 8:00 p.m. Eastern time on October 16, 2026.",
        "Submit the completed application package in the GRFP Application Module by 8:00 p.m. Eastern time on the deadline for your Major Field of Study (October 19, 20, 22 or 23, 2026).",
        "Track your application status in the GRFP Application Module; NSF typically notifies applicants in the spring."
      ],
      "selection_notes": "Applications are reviewed by disciplinary and interdisciplinary scientists, engineers and STEM experts selected by NSF Program Officers, with care taken to ensure no conflicts of interest. Applications are reviewed in broad areas of related disciplines based on the Major Field of Study selected by the applicant, using the two NSF Merit Review Criteria: Intellectual Merit (potential to advance knowledge) and Broader Impacts (potential to benefit society and contribute to specific, desired societal outcomes). Intellectual Merit factors include the ability to plan and conduct research, work independently, work as part of a team, interpret and communicate research, take initiative, think creatively, solve problems, persist and overcome challenges, and demonstrated intellectual ability as indicated by grades, coursework and awards. Broader Impacts factors include increasing participation in STEM, improving STEM education through outreach and mentoring, increasing public scientific literacy, increasing collaboration among academia and industry, improving educator development, improving societal well-being, developing a globally competitive STEM workforce, improving national security, increasing U.S. economic competitiveness, and enhancing research and education infrastructure. GRFP was established to recruit and support individuals who demonstrate the potential to make significant contributions in STEM.",
      "notes": "No post-graduate study service requirement. Fellowship funds are awarded to the institution of higher education at which the fellow is enrolled, and the institution is responsible for disbursing the stipend to the fellow; the fellowship is therefore only usable at an eligible accredited nonprofit U.S. institution. The fellowship period is five years with three years of financial support, so fellows must plan for two unfunded years. Fellows may transfer to another institution after completing the first fellowship year. Practice-oriented professional degree programs (medical, dental, law, public health, MBA, MPH, MSW, JD, MD, DVM, DDS) are excluded at any time during the fellowship, as are joint science-professional degree programs. Applicants may submit only one application per annual competition, and only in the first year of their first graduate degree program (joint bachelor's-master's students are limited to one application). No clinical or medical-school pathway is eligible.",
      "image_url": "https://www.nsf.gov/themes/custom/nsf_theme/components/molecules/logo/logo-desktop.png",
      "image_source_url": "https://www.nsf.gov/funding/initiatives/grfp",
      "image_source_type": "official_government",
      "image_verified_at": "2026-10-01",
      "image_alt_text": "United States National Science Foundation logo",
      "detail": {
        "official_details": {
          "description": "The purpose of the U.S. National Science Foundation Graduate Research Fellowship Program (GRFP) is to help ensure the quality, vitality, and strength of the scientific and engineering workforce of the United States. Since 1952, the program recognizes and supports outstanding graduate students pursuing full-time research-based master's and doctoral degrees in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) fields. GRFP was established to recruit and support individuals who demonstrate the potential to make significant contributions in STEM. Each Fellowship provides three years of support over a five-year Fellowship period. For each of the three years of support, NSF provides a $37,000 stipend and a $16,000 Cost of Education allowance provided to the institution of higher education (IHE) in lieu of all tuition and mandatory fees for each Fellow using the support in a Fellowship year. While the Fellowship is offered to the individual, the Fellowship funds are awarded to the IHE at which a Fellow is enrolled, and the IHE is responsible for disbursement of the stipend to the Fellow.",
          "purpose": [
            "Help ensure the quality, vitality, and strength of the scientific and engineering workforce of the United States.",
            "Support outstanding graduate students in NSF-supported STEM disciplines who are pursuing research-based master's and doctoral degrees at accredited, nonprofit U.S. institutions of higher education.",
            "Support outstanding graduate students pursuing research-based degrees.",
            "Increase participation in science, technology, education, and mathematics (STEM).",
            "Build the next generation of scientific leaders.",
            "Recruit and support individuals who demonstrate the potential to make significant contributions in STEM.",
            "Recognize the potential of individuals based on their demonstrated potential for significant achievements in science and engineering, rather than funding specific projects."
          ],
          "sdg_requirement": false,
          "sdg_requirement_details": [],
          "degree_levels": [
            "Master's (research-based)",
            "PhD (research-based)"
          ],
          "study_type": [
            "Full-time enrollment in a research-based graduate degree program",
            "Research-based STEM study in an eligible Major Field of Study"
          ],
          "degree_award": "Full graduate degree (research-based master's or doctoral). The GRFP itself is not a degree; the fellowship funds study toward a degree at an eligible institution.",
          "duration": "Five-year award with three years of financial support. $37,000 stipend plus $16,000 cost of education (COE) paid to the institution for each year of funding support; $53,000 each year for three years over a five-year period.",
          "application_cycle": "Annual competition. The GRFP Application Module usually opens in July or August. For the 2027 competition (solicitation NSF 26-526) reference letters are due October 16, 2026 and applications are due October 19-23, 2026 depending on Major Field of Study.",
          "deadline_central": "2026-10-16",
          "deadline_central_display": "October 16, 2026 by 8:00 p.m. Eastern time (all reference letters due). Application deadlines follow by field of study.",
          "deadline_institutional": "None published. Applications are submitted directly by the applicant in the GRFP Application Module rather than through an institutional office.",
          "deadline_note": "The deadline depends on the applicant's Major Field of Study: October 19, 2026 for Life Sciences; October 20, 2026 for Computer and Information Science and Engineering, Materials Research, Psychology, Social Sciences; October 22, 2026 for Engineering; October 23, 2026 for Chemistry, Geosciences, Mathematical Sciences, Physics and Astronomy. All applications must be received by 8:00 p.m. Eastern Time on the applicable deadline date; the module does not accept applications after the deadline. November 15, 2026 is the last day to withdraw an application and have it not count towards the number of times an individual may apply.",
          "status": "OPEN",
          "funding_type": "Stipend plus tuition and mandatory fee coverage via cost-of-education allowance",
          "funding_label": "$37,000 stipend + $16,000 cost of education allowance per year, for three years over a five-year fellowship period",
          "funding_amount": 37000,
          "funding_currency": "USD",
          "funding_period": "per year of funding support (three years of support within a five-year fellowship period); $53,000 total per year including the COE allowance",
          "tuition_coverage": "Full tuition and mandatory fees. NSF provides a $16,000 Cost of Education allowance to the institution of higher education in lieu of all tuition and mandatory fees for each Fellow using the support in a fellowship year. The COE payment covers all tuition and mandatory fees (no cost to student).",
          "living_cost_coverage": "A $37,000 annual stipend paid to the fellow for each of the three years of financial support. The pages read do not publish a separate housing, meals or books allowance.",
          "travel_coverage": null,
          "fully_funded": true,
          "tuition_rule": "The $16,000 cost-of-education allowance is provided to the institution of higher education (IHE) in lieu of all tuition and mandatory fees for each fellow using the support in a fellowship year, so there is no tuition or mandatory-fee cost to the student. Because the fellowship is offered to the individual but the funds are awarded to the IHE, the institution is responsible for disbursement of the stipend to the fellow.",
          "home_tuition_rule": null,
          "host_institution_fee": "No fee payable to the host is published. The cost-of-education allowance is paid to the institution in lieu of all tuition and mandatory fees.",
          "tax_status": null,
          "covered_expenses": [
            "All tuition and mandatory fees, through the $16,000 annual cost-of-education allowance paid to the institution of higher education.",
            "A $37,000 annual stipend paid to the fellow for each of the three years of support."
          ],
          "not_covered_or_excluded": [
            "The fellowship period is five years but only three years carry financial support, so fellows must fund two years of the fellowship period themselves.",
            "No published coverage for health insurance, room and board, books, computers, or travel on the pages read; these costs must be met from the $37,000 stipend or other sources.",
            "No international or overseas travel or placement funding is published.",
            "Practice-oriented professional degrees are excluded entirely (medical, dental, law, public health, MBA, MPH, MSW, JD, MD, DVM, DDS).",
            "GRE scores are not part of the application and are not funded or required.",
            "Applicants cannot obtain copies of past successful applications from NSF."
          ],
          "benefits": [
            "Access to professional development opportunities and the NSF GRFP community.",
            "Access to supplemental funding to sustain research while on medical leave.",
            "No post-graduate study service requirement.",
            "Portability: the fellowship can be transferred to a different institution of higher education; fellows can transfer to another institution after completion of the first fellowship year.",
            "Reputation and network effects: GRFP alumni include over 40 Nobel Prize recipients and over 450 members of the National Academy of Sciences."
          ],
          "eligibility": [
            "At the time of submission, must be a U.S. citizen, national, or a permanent resident (\"green-card\" holder).",
            "Must intend to enroll or be enrolled full-time in an eligible research-based master's or doctoral degree program in an eligible field of study in STEM.",
            "Must never have previously accepted an NSF Graduate Research Fellowship.",
            "Must have declined any previously offered NSF Graduate Research Fellowship by the deadline (if applicable).",
            "Must have completed less than one academic year in a graduate degree program according to the institution's academic calendar; non-degree coursework must be clearly identified in the transcript and does not count toward this limit.",
            "Must not be a current NSF employee.",
            "Degree programs must be at a nonprofit institution of higher education accredited in, and having a campus located in, the United States, its territories, or possessions, or the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico.",
            "An eligible applicant can submit only one application per annual competition.",
            "Select the Major Field of Study and subfield most closely aligned with the proposed graduate program of study and research plan; if a subfield is not listed in the Appendix of the Notice of Funding Opportunity, it is not eligible.",
            "Interdisciplinary research is allowed, but the application is reviewed based on the first Major Field of Study selected.",
            "By signing and submitting the application, the applicant certifies meeting the NSF GRFP eligibility criteria; willful provision of false information is a criminal offense under U.S. Code, Title 18, Section 1001."
          ],
          "general_eligibility": [
            "One application per annual competition.",
            "Full-time enrollment in an eligible research-based graduate degree program.",
            "U.S. citizenship, nationality or permanent residency required.",
            "The applicant questionnaire on the eligibility page is a tool only and is not an official determination of an individual's eligibility; the Notice of Funding Opportunity contains the official eligibility guidelines."
          ],
          "ineligibility_conditions": [
            "Individuals who are not U.S. citizens, U.S. nationals, or permanent residents, including foreign nationals in the U.S. on a student visa and those awaiting green cards.",
            "Individuals who do not intend to enroll or be enrolled in a research-based graduate degree program in an eligible field of study in STEM.",
            "Individuals who have previously accepted an NSF Graduate Research Fellowship offer.",
            "Individuals who were awarded the fellowship and did not notify NSF of their intention to accept or decline by the published deadline for accepting the fellowship.",
            "Individuals who have previously applied while enrolled in a degree-granting graduate program.",
            "Individuals who have earned a master's, doctoral, or terminal degree in any field.",
            "Individuals who are current NSF employees.",
            "Individuals who will be enrolled in a practice-oriented professional degree program such as medical, dental, law, and public health degrees at any time during the fellowship, including but not limited to MBA, MPH, MSW, JD, MD, DVM, and DDS programs.",
            "Individuals who plan to pursue a joint science-professional degree program (such as an MD/Ph.D. or JD/Ph.D.) even if proposing to use the GRFP only for the Ph.D. part; also applicants enrolled in, or planning to enroll in, a graduate degree program while on a leave of absence from a professional degree program or professional degree-graduate degree joint program.",
            "Applicants who completed more than one academic year of graduate study: all enrollment in graduate degree-granting programs counts toward the limit regardless of field of study, including study in non-NSF-supported fields.",
            "Applicants who hold a master's degree, even if in the first year of a Ph.D. program.",
            "Applicants whose statement documents are not compliant with the published formatting requirements: documents that are not compliant will not be accepted by the GRFP Application Module.",
            "Applications submitted after the deadline, or submitted by email or physical mail, are not accepted."
          ],
          "citizenship_residency": "U.S. citizens, U.S. nationals, or permanent resident (\"green-card\") holders only. Foreign nationals on student visas and those awaiting green cards are not eligible. The degree program must be at a nonprofit institution of higher education accredited in, and having a campus located in, the United States, its territories, or possessions, or the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico.",
          "international_eligibility": "None. The program is restricted to U.S. citizens, U.S. nationals and permanent residents studying at accredited nonprofit institutions with a campus in the U.S., its territories or possessions, or Puerto Rico.",
          "academic_level_rules": {
            "undergraduate": [
              "Level 1: Undergraduate students in the final (senior) year of a bachelor's degree program.",
              "Level 1: Bachelor's degree holders with no enrollment in a graduate degree program (non-degree graduate coursework allowed).",
              "No restrictions on the number of times an undergraduate senior in a bachelor's degree program, or a bachelor's degree holder with no prior graduate degree enrollment, can apply before enrolling in a graduate degree-granting program."
            ],
            "masters": [
              "Level 2: Joint bachelor's-master's degree students in the final undergraduate or first graduate year; they must be in the final undergraduate or first graduate year of the joint degree program as of the application deadline.",
              "Joint bachelor's-master's degree students are considered graduate students and can apply only once.",
              "First-year graduate students in their first graduate degree program with less than one academic year completed in the degree program may apply (a research-based master's degree program is eligible).",
              "Applicants who have earned a master's degree in any field are not eligible."
            ],
            "phd": [
              "Level 2: First-year graduate students in their first graduate degree program with less than one academic year completed according to the institution's academic calendar.",
              "Individuals enrolled in graduate degree programs can apply only once, in the first year of their first graduate program.",
              "Doctoral students who have completed more than one academic year of graduate study, or who previously earned a master's, doctoral or terminal degree, are not eligible."
            ]
          },
          "host_requirements": [
            "The institution must be a nonprofit institution of higher education accredited in, and having a campus located in, the United States, its territories, or possessions, or the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico.",
            "The Fellowship funds are awarded to the institution of higher education at which the Fellow is enrolled, and the IHE is responsible for disbursement of the stipend to the Fellow.",
            "The fellowship may be transferred to a different institution of higher education, and fellows can transfer after completion of the first fellowship year.",
            "Recipients are covered by the GRFP Administrative Guide for fellows and coordinating officials."
          ],
          "requirements": [
            "Prepare and submit the application in the GRFP Application Module on Research.gov; the module usually opens in July or August.",
            "Create a Research.gov account if you do not already have one.",
            "Provide accurate personal information: full legal name, mailing address, permanent address, date of birth, high school location, demographic information.",
            "Select Level 1 (Undergraduate) or Level 2 (Graduate) in the application.",
            "Provide the list of institutions, enrollment status, enrollment dates and degree conferred (if applicable) for all degree-granting undergraduate and graduate institutions attended.",
            "Provide significant academic honors, fellowships, scholarships, publications and presentations.",
            "Select the Major Field of Study, which determines the application deadline and the reviewing panel.",
            "Request up to five reference letters and rank them in order of preference; three reference writer names and contact information are required and two letters must be received. Family members cannot be reference writers. Reference writers are notified by email of the request along with instructions.",
            "Prepare the Personal, Relevant Background, and Future Goals Statement with headings for Intellectual Merit and Broader Impacts, maximum three pages.",
            "Prepare the Graduate Research Plan Statement with headings for Intellectual Merit and Broader Impacts, maximum two pages; it should include the research idea and proposed approach, resources needed, potential to advance knowledge, and potential to benefit society.",
            "Both statements must be in PDF format and comply with the page size, margin, font and spacing requirements.",
            "Submit only through the GRFP Application Module; emailed and/or physically mailed applications will not be accepted.",
            "You may revise your application until the deadline, but can make no changes after the deadline passes."
          ],
          "documents": [
            "Personal, Relevant Background, and Future Goals Statement (maximum three pages).",
            "Graduate Research Plan (maximum two pages).",
            "Official undergraduate academic transcripts.",
            "Graduate transcripts, if applicable; these may be unofficial if the institution of higher education does not provide official transcripts for first-year, first-academic-term graduate students.",
            "Two reference letters (two must be received by NSF for the application to be reviewed; the module permits requesting up to five, and only the top three are considered if more than three are received).",
            "Three reference writer names and contact information.",
            "Completed application package."
          ],
          "document_format_rules": [
            "PDF format only for the statement documents.",
            "Official transcripts should be uploaded in PDF format.",
            "Page limits: Personal, Relevant Background, and Future Goals Statement three pages; Graduate Research Plan two pages. The page limits include all references, citations, charts, figures, images, and lists of publications and presentations.",
            "Page size: standard 8.5\" x 11\" page size.",
            "Margins: 1\" margins on all sides, with no text inside the 1\" margins (no header, footer, name, or page number). Remove page numbers; stray images or shapes can violate the margin rule even when the page margin is set to 1 inch.",
            "Fonts: Arial (not Arial Narrow), Courier New, or Palatino Linotype in 10-point or larger; Times New Roman or Computer Modern family, 11-point or larger. Symbol or Cambria Math fonts are accepted for Greek letters or special characters. Smaller than 10-point fonts are accepted for mathematical formulas, equations, figures, tables or diagram captions.",
            "Spacing: no less than single-spacing (approximately 6 lines per inch). Do not use line spacing options such as \"exactly 11 point,\" that are less than single spaced.",
            "Equations and symbols should be inserted as a non-editable image to bypass compliance checking and avoid font type and size errors.",
            "Provide descriptive alternative text (alt text) for all images, graphs and charts, and use sufficient colour contrast for legibility.",
            "Do not include links to supplemental materials (papers, videos); reviewers are instructed to evaluate only the evidence provided within the application.",
            "Google Docs may not properly export bulleted text and should not be used; OpenOffice inserts an unallowable font for superscripts and subscripts; certain LaTeX-generated PDF elements are not yet supported and LaTeX fonts in formulas are not supported; Microsoft Word 2007 or earlier may produce font errors.",
            "In Microsoft Office, \"Select All\" does not include inserted images, shapes, numbered lists or bullets, which carry their own fonts and must be updated to accepted fonts and sizes.",
            "If text is inside an image, it must be part of the image and not editable, otherwise font errors can result.",
            "Do not use 'Print as PDF'; use 'Export' / 'Create PDF'. On Mac, avoid 'Save as PDF' set to 'Best for Printing'; choose 'Best for electronic distribution and accessibility'.",
            "To check fonts: open the PDF, right click the document content, select 'Document Properties', open the 'Fonts' tab and review the font types.",
            "Blank pages within a PDF cause upload errors; export the PDF to Word, delete the blank page and re-save as PDF.",
            "Applications are automatically checked by the GRFP Application Module for compliance; documents that are not compliant will not be accepted."
          ],
          "selection_criteria": [
            "Intellectual Merit: the potential to advance knowledge.",
            "Broader Impacts: the potential to benefit society and contribute to the achievement of specific, desired societal outcomes."
          ],
          "selection_notes": "Applications are reviewed by disciplinary and interdisciplinary scientists, engineers, and experts in STEM fields. Reviewers are selected by NSF Program Officers charged with oversight of the review process, and care is taken to ensure that reviewers have no conflicts of interest with the applicants. Applications are reviewed in broad areas of related disciplines based on the Major Field of Study the applicant selected, using all available information in the completed application. Intellectual Merit factors may include the applicant's ability to plan and conduct research, work independently, work as a part of a team, interpret and communicate research (e.g. through publications or presentations), take initiative, think creatively, solve problems, persist and overcome challenges, and demonstrated intellectual ability as indicated by grades, coursework and awards. Broader Impacts factors may include the likelihood that the applicant's activities will increase participation in STEM, improve STEM education in schools through outreach and mentoring, increase public scientific literacy and public engagement with STEM through community outreach, increase collaboration among academia, industry and other types of organizations, improve education and educator development at any level in STEM, improve the well-being of individuals in society, develop a globally competitive STEM workforce, improve national security, increase the economic competitiveness of the U.S., and enhance infrastructure for research and education. NSF does not provide past applications or statements. GRFP funds people, not specific projects; the plan demonstrates potential, not a binding commitment. The program is highly competitive. NSF typically notifies applicants in the spring. Estimated number of awards under the 2027 solicitation: 1500, pending availability of funds.",
          "application_method": [
            "Create a Research.gov account if you do not already have one.",
            "Access the GRFP Application Module on Research.gov, which usually opens in July or August.",
            "Enter accurate personal information.",
            "Select your eligibility level.",
            "Enter education, work and other experience and upload transcripts.",
            "Select the Major Field of Study and subfield.",
            "Enter three reference writers' details, rank them and submit the requests.",
            "Upload the Personal, Relevant Background, and Future Goals Statement and the Graduate Research Plan Statement as compliant PDFs.",
            "Ensure at least two reference letters arrive by 8:00 p.m. Eastern time on October 16, 2026.",
            "Submit the application package before 8:00 p.m. Eastern time on the deadline for your Major Field of Study.",
            "Track status in the GRFP Application Module."
          ],
          "application_steps": [
            "Create a Research.gov account if you do not already have one.",
            "Access the GRFP Application Module, which usually opens in July or August.",
            "Provide accurate personal information: full legal name, mailing address, permanent address, date of birth, high school location, demographic information.",
            "Select Level 1 (Undergraduate) or Level 2 (Graduate).",
            "List all degree-granting undergraduate and graduate institutions attended with enrollment status, enrollment dates and degree conferred (if applicable).",
            "Upload official undergraduate transcripts in PDF format; add graduate transcripts where applicable.",
            "List significant academic honors, fellowships, scholarships, publications and presentations.",
            "Select the Major Field of Study and subfield most closely aligned with the proposed graduate program and research plan.",
            "Enter the name, organizational affiliation and email address of three reference writers and rank them in order of preference; submit the request so writers receive email notification with instructions. Family members cannot be reference writers.",
            "Upload the Personal, Relevant Background, and Future Goals Statement (three pages maximum, PDF only, with Intellectual Merit and Broader Impacts headings).",
            "Upload the Graduate Research Plan Statement (two pages maximum, PDF only, with Intellectual Merit and Broader Impacts headings).",
            "Track confirmation that at least two reference letters have been received by 8:00 p.m. Eastern time on October 16, 2026.",
            "Submit the application by 8:00 p.m. Eastern time on the deadline for the selected Major Field of Study.",
            "If you need to withdraw or restore an unsubmitted or submitted package, use the 'Withdraw Application Package' / 'Restore Application Package' options in the Application Package Optional Task List on the Welcome Applicants homepage. To update a submitted application before the deadline, withdraw, restore, then 'Prepare Application', make the updates and resubmit."
          ],
          "applicant_submission_route": [
            "The applicant submits the application personally through the GRFP Application Module on Research.gov.",
            "No institutional submission route is published; applications are submitted by the applicant, and emailed or physically mailed applications are not accepted.",
            "Reference letters are submitted directly by the reference writers in response to the applicant's electronic request; the module notifies writers by email with instructions."
