      "application_period": "Online application opens August 3, 2026 and closes 8:00pm Eastern on November 5, 2026 for the 2027-2028 programme. Semi-finalist notifications with travel arrangements are made by February 24, 2027; interviews in Washington, DC February 27 - March 1, 2027; finalist notifications March 9, 2027; congressional fellows placement interviews mid-June 2027; fellows arrive in Washington, DC mid-August 2027.",
      "status": "open",
      "is_verified": true,
      "last_verified_date": "2026-10-01",
      "description": "The AEF Programme places accomplished K-12 STEM educators for eleven months in federal agencies or U.S. Congressional offices, working on national education programmes and policy. Each fellow receives a monthly living stipend of $8,200, a travel allowance of up to $5,000 for approved travel requested by the host agency, an education allowance of up to $1,000 for approved professional development expenses, relocation reimbursement of up to $3,500 for fellows relocating from outside the Washington, DC area, and a health insurance stipend of up to $550 per month on an individual plan or $1,300 per month on a family plan. The DOE sponsors up to five congressional office placements. The programme is a single eleven-month appointment; it is not a degree award and involves no tuition.",
      "program_type": "Government fellowship",
      "best_fit": "Experienced, currently employed U.S. K-12 STEM teachers or district officials who have at least five complete years of full-time classroom STEM teaching, can secure an eleven-month leave of absence, and can relocate to the Washington, DC area.",
      "funding_amount": 8200,
      "funding_currency": "USD",
      "funding_period": "monthly living stipend over an eleven-month fellowship",
      "eligibility_summary": "Applicants must be U.S. citizens who have completed five years of full-time STEM classroom teaching, have taught full-time in a U.S. public or private elementary or secondary school or school district for at least five of the last seven years, are currently so employed, can obtain an eleven-month leave of absence, and can obtain a recommendation letter from a school district official.",
      "eligibility": [
        "Be a U.S. citizen at the time of applying (by the November 5, 2026 application deadline).",
        "Have a minimum of five complete years of full-time classroom teaching experience prior to submitting the application; student teaching does not count toward this minimum full-time teaching requirement.",
        "Have been teaching full-time in a public or private elementary or secondary school for at least five of the last seven years in a science, technology, engineering, or mathematics (STEM) discipline; current employment may be as an educator or district official.",
        "Be currently employed full-time in a public or private elementary or secondary school or school district in the U.S.",
        "Be able to provide a letter of recommendation from a school district official as one of their three required letters of recommendation.",
        "Be able to obtain a leave of absence from teaching in order to participate in the program for the full eleven months."
      ],
      "coverage": [
        "Monthly living stipend of $8,200.",
        "Travel allowance of up to $5,000 for approved travel requested by the host agency; limitations on foreign travel vary by agency.",
