      "selection_notes": "Applications are sifted against the eligibility criteria from October 2026, then independent reading committees assess all eligible applications from mid-October 2026 to January 2027. British embassies and high commissions produce shortlists and interview candidates in March to April 2027; results are announced from mid-June 2027. Applicants are assessed on leadership and influencing skills, the ability to build professional relationships to achieve positive outcomes, the reasons for the course and university choice (linked to UK priorities such as promoting growth and prosperity, building climate resilience, strengthening security and stability, and supporting development for more inclusive and effective societies), and a realistic short, mid and long-term career plan. For the ASEAN element specifically, 'the highest-ranking applicant(s) from each participating country will be considered'. Chevening states: 'The use of AI software to generate answers in Chevening applications is strictly prohibited.'",
      "notes": "Applicants must commit to returning to their home country (country of award) for at least two years after the scholarship ends. The award is for one year only and is not renewable; Chevening publishes no renewal or deferral provisions, so those fields are left null. Reference letters must be uploaded at least seven working days before the interview - 'Failure to do so will result in your application no longer being considered for a Chevening award.' Applicants must not have previously studied in the UK on a UK government-funded scholarship (a former Chevening Fellow may apply for a scholarship only after five years have passed since completing their fellowship). No clinical or medical restriction is published.",
