            "Any injury, accident, illness or loss of personal property: 'Neither the FCDO nor the Secretariat assumes responsibility for any injury, accident, illness, loss of personal property or other contingencies which may befall a Fellow during the course of their Chevening Award.'"
          ],
          "benefits": [
            "Six integrated programme themes: Global Leadership in an Era of Complexity; Understanding People, Culture, and Organisations; Cyber Resilience and Information Threats; Artificial Intelligence, Governance, and Emerging Technology; Strategic Decision-Making Under Pressure; Building Resilience and Future Leadership",
            "Lectures, workshops, simulations, practitioner speakers, research activity, UK institutional visits, and opportunities to build enduring international professional networks",
            "Academic support in developing a research project, and presentation of findings to the FCDO in London",
            "Invitation to the Chevening welcome reception and/or induction on arrival in the UK",
            "Invitation to a feedback session at the completion of the award",
            "Membership of the global Chevening alumni network"
          ],
          "eligibility": [
            "Be a citizen of and resident in one of the 35 listed European countries at the point of application: Andorra, Austria, Belgium, Bulgaria, Croatia, Cyprus, Czechia, Denmark, Estonia, Finland, France, Germany, Gibraltar, Greece, Holy See, Hungary, Iceland, Ireland, Italy, Latvia, Liechtenstein, Lithuania, Luxembourg, Malta, Monaco, the Netherlands, Norway, Poland, Portugal, Romania, San Marino, Slovakia, Slovenia, Spain, Sweden, Switzerland.",
            "If you live in a European overseas territory, apply through the relevant Chevening award scheme for the European country of which you are a citizen.",
            "Hold a postgraduate qualification (or equivalent professional training or experience in a relevant field).",
            "Have at least five years' relevant professional experience (7,200 hours).",
            "Be a mid-career professional with responsibilities for national or international security, employed within government, public or private sector, academia, defence, security, law enforcement, intelligence, civil protection, resilience, or an international organisation.",
            "Be working in a role with the potential to influence organisational capability, policy, strategy, or operational effectiveness within their organisation and/or country.",
            "Demonstrate leadership potential and a commitment to applying the knowledge and networks gained through the fellowship on completion.",
            "Be a citizen of a fellowship-eligible country or territory; demonstrate the potential to rise to a position of leadership and influence and the personal, intellectual and interpersonal attributes reflecting this potential; return to your country of citizenship for at least one year after the fellowship ends; and meet all programme-specific requirements."
          ],
          "general_eligibility": [
            "Chevening Fellowships are aimed at mid-career professionals who have reached a position of influence and want to increase their knowledge, networks, and potential through tailored short courses, research, or professional placements at UK institutions.",
            "Fellows are often people of significant standing in their home countries and will usually have completed some element(s) of higher education already.",
            "Eligible work experience types include full-time employment, part-time employment, voluntary work, and paid or unpaid internships.",
            "If you have held more than fifteen positions, enter the periods which make up the greatest number of hours worked.",
            "You do not need to resign from your work position, but it is your responsibility to ensure your employer will release you.",
            "Chevening Fellowship programmes typically have varying eligibility requirements; programme-specific requirements are on individual programme pages."
          ],
          "ineligibility_conditions": [
            "Hold British or dual British citizenship (unless you are a citizen of a British Overseas Territory).",
            "Have received UK Government scholarship funding (including Chevening) within the last five years - 'You will only be eligible to apply for a Chevening Fellowship after a period of five years following the completion of your first UK Government-funded scholarship.'",
            "Be an employee, a former employee, or relative of an employee of His Majesty's Government, or have been within the last two years from the opening of Chevening applications (including the FCDO, all British Embassies/High Commissions, the Home Office, the Ministry of Defence, the Department for Business, Energy and Industrial Strategy, the Department for International Trade and the UKVI), the British Council, or a sponsoring UK university. Individuals contracted to work on any British Council programme or project, and their relatives, are ineligible.",
            "Employees, former employees, or relatives of an employee of Chevening partner organisations may apply, but if that employment took place within the last two years you cannot receive a Chevening partner award from the organisation you work for, previously worked for, or are affiliated with through relatives.",
            "Not be a citizen AND resident of one of the 35 listed European countries at the point of application - the fellowship is open to all non-ODA eligible European countries only.",
            "Not meet the five-year (7,200 hour) work experience minimum: 'If you do not already have the level of work experience required for your chosen fellowship programme, you will be unable to submit your application.'",
            "Immediate relatives are defined as parents or step-parents, siblings or step-siblings, children or step-children, spouse, civil partner or unmarried (where the couple have been in a relationship akin to marriage or civil partnership for at least two years)."
          ],
          "citizenship_residency": "Citizens of, and residents of, one of 35 named non-ODA eligible European countries: Andorra, Austria, Belgium, Bulgaria, Croatia, Cyprus, Czechia, Denmark, Estonia, Finland, France, Germany, Gibraltar, Greece, Holy See, Hungary, Iceland, Ireland, Italy, Latvia, Liechtenstein, Lithuania, Luxembourg, Malta, Monaco, the Netherlands, Norway, Poland, Portugal, Romania, San Marino, Slovakia, Slovenia, Spain, Sweden and Switzerland. The programme is open to all non-ODA eligible European countries.",
          "international_eligibility": "Explicitly for non-UK international applicants. British and dual British citizens are barred, recent UK government scholarship recipients are barred for five years, and both citizenship and residence in a listed European country are required at the point of application.",
