      "deadline": "Applications for entry in the academic year 2027/8 are now open. Two rounds. US citizens resident in the USA must apply in October - Application deadline Wednesday 14th October 2026 (Full application must be submitted by 11:59pm UK time on the date given). All other eligible applicants - Application deadline dependent on your course, either Tuesday 8th December 2026 or Wednesday 6th January 2027 (Full application must be submitted by 11:59pm UK time).",
      "deadline_date": "2026-10-14",
      "deadline_precision": "day",
      "application_period": "Applications opened 11th September 2026 for entry in the academic year 2027/8. Round 1 (US citizens resident in the USA only): open 11 September 2026, deadline 14 October 2026. Round 2 (all other eligible applicants): open 11 September 2026, deadline set by the Course Funding Deadline for the specific course, either 8 December 2026 or 6 January 2027.",
      "status": "open",
      "is_verified": true,
      "last_verified_date": "2026-10-01",
      "description": "The Gates Cambridge Scholarship is a full-cost postgraduate scholarship at the University of Cambridge for applicants from outside the United Kingdom. It pays the University Composition Fee, a maintenance allowance, one economy return single airfare, inbound visa costs and the Immigration Health Surcharge, plus discretionary funds. It is open to any subject at Cambridge, which includes health-related postgraduate study such as the PhD in Public Health and Primary Care, although the clinical medical degrees are explicitly excluded. Approximately 70 full-cost scholarships are awarded each year, about two-thirds to PhD students.",
      "program_type": "Foundation scholarship",
      "best_fit": "Postgraduate applicants from outside the UK who are academically outstanding, can show a commitment to improving the lives of others and demonstrate leadership potential, and who want a health, medicine, public health or life-science related postgraduate degree at Cambridge.",
      "funding_amount": null,
      "funding_currency": null,
      "funding_period": null,
      "eligibility_summary": "Citizens of any country outside the United Kingdom applying to a Cambridge PhD (full-time or part-time), MLitt (full-time) or one-year full-time postgraduate course, with undergraduate degrees, MASt courses, most part-time degrees and a named list of professional degrees excluded.",
      "eligibility": [
        "a citizen of any country outside the United Kingdom",
        "applying to pursue a PhD (full-time or part-time) at the University of Cambridge",
        "applying to pursue an MLitt (full-time) at the University of Cambridge",
        "applying to pursue a one-year postgraduate course (full-time) at the University of Cambridge, with some exceptions",
        "Applicants currently studying at Cambridge can apply for a Gates Cambridge Scholarship for a new postgraduate course; for example a student currently on an MPhil can apply for a Gates Cambridge Scholarship to pursue a PhD",
        "Current Gates Cambridge Scholars may also apply for a second scholarship if they are applying for a new degree",
        "All current Cambridge students will be considered in the second, international round"
      ],
      "coverage": [
        "the University Composition Fee at the appropriate rate",
        "a maintenance allowance for a single student (£23,152 for 12 months at the 2026-27 rate; pro rata for courses shorter than 12 months) - for PhD scholars the award is for up to 4 years",
        "one economy single airfare at both the beginning and end of the course",
        "inbound visa costs and the cost of the Immigration Health Surcharge"
      ],
      "benefits": [
        "academic development funding - from up to £750 to up to £4,000, dependent on the length of your course, to attend conferences and courses",
        "Dependent Children Allowance - up to £12,793 per annum for one child and up to £18,244 per annum for two or more children",
        "fieldwork - you may apply to keep up to your normal maintenance allowance while on fieldwork as part of your PhD",
        "maternity/paternity funding - you may apply to intermit your studies for up to 6 months and continue to receive your maintenance allowance during this time",
        "hardship funding for unforeseen difficulties facing the scholar"
      ],
      "requirements": [
        "Complete the admission to a course and a College place section and the Gates Cambridge part of the funding section of the University of Cambridge Graduate Application Portal",
        "Answer four Gates Cambridge selection questions in the funding application form (academic excellence approx. 200 words / up to 1,400 characters; reasons for the specific course approx. 200 words / up to 1,400 characters; commitment to improving the lives of others approx. 300 words / up to 2,100 characters; capacity for leadership approx. 300 words / up to 2,100 characters)",
        "PhD applicants must submit a research proposal as part of the application for funding",
        "Arrange two academic references for admission plus an additional Gates Cambridge reference",
        "Ensure both academic and Gates Cambridge referees submit their references by the specified deadline"
      ],
      "english_requirement": "No single programme-wide Gates Cambridge rule is published. The University of Cambridge states: 'If English is not your first language, you may need to show that you meet the English language requirement for your course', and 'For most courses, you don't need to provide your language test results until you're made an offer.' Course-specific minimum scores are set on each course page in the University Course Directory.",
      "application_method": [
        "Search the University of Cambridge Course Directory and note the Course Funding Deadline for your specific course",
        "Contact prospective supervisors and, for PhD applications, agree the research proposal topic",
        "Register on the University of Cambridge Graduate Applicant Portal (apply.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk)",
        "Complete the admission section: the specific graduate course and a College place, with academic background, plans and suitability",
        "Complete the funding section and the Gates Cambridge sub-section, including the four selection questions (and the research proposal for PhD applicants)",
        "Upload the supporting documents as scanned originals to the Applicant Portal",
        "Nominate two academic referees and one Gates Cambridge referee, briefing the Gates Cambridge referee fully on the selection criteria",
        "Submit the application by the Course Funding Deadline; references must be uploaded by your referees through the Referee Portal by the same deadline",
        "Wait for departmental nomination; if nominated, attend the 25-30 minute panel interview"
      ],
      "selection_notes": "Two-stage selection. Stage 1: academic departments at Cambridge nominate a small number of eligible applicants who meet all four criteria to a very high level; only departmentally nominated applicants are invited to interview. Stage 2: a 25-30 minute interview with one of four subject-based panels (Arts, Biological Sciences, Physical Sciences, Social Sciences) consisting of academics and practitioners, and possibly Gates Cambridge alumni and a Trust representative. The interview has four sections: welcome and introduction; Gates Cambridge (knowledge of the scheme, motivation, why you could be a Scholar, thoughts on the opportunities and responsibilities); academic and career plans; final thoughts and questions. Scholars are selected after interview. Gates Cambridge states it cannot provide feedback to unsuccessful applicants.",
      "notes": "Not covered: bench fees and the costs of scientific equipment or similar academic resources - the Trust expects such core course costs to be covered by the Cambridge academic department. No funding is provided for a partner under the Dependent Children Allowance. The Trust does not fund other fieldwork costs; these should be funded by the University Composition Fee. Where a student has already gained a fee award from public authorities (such as UKRI) they should accept it, and the Trust will not pay their fee or may share their fee and maintenance costs. If the applicant is receiving a salary from an employer or has another substantial scholarship, the Trust reserves the right to reduce or not pay the standard maintenance allowance, and must be told ASAP of any other funding awarded towards the degree. Deferred entry and part-time study: a pilot continues to allow applications to fund a part-time doctoral degree; part-time degrees other than the PhD are not considered. University rules bar a second degree of the same type from Cambridge.",
      "image_url": "https://www.gatescambridge.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/GC-logo-positive_font_update_WHITE_OUT.png",
      "image_source_url": "https://www.gatescambridge.org/apply/timeline/",
      "image_source_type": "official_organization",
      "image_verified_at": "2026-10-01",
      "image_alt_text": "Gates Cambridge",
      "detail": {
        "official_details": {
          "description": "The Gates Cambridge Scholarship covers the full cost of studying at Cambridge and provides additional discretionary funding. Core components are the University Composition Fee, a maintenance allowance for a single student, one economy return single airfare, and inbound visa costs and the Immigration Health Surcharge. Around 70 full-cost scholarships are awarded each year from an application pool of approximately 850 applications.",
          "purpose": [
            "The Gates Cambridge mission is to build a global network of future leaders committed to improving the lives of others.",
            "Each year Gates Cambridge offers around 70 full-cost scholarships to outstanding applicants from countries outside the UK to pursue a postgraduate degree in any subject available at the University of Cambridge.",
            "Approximately two-thirds of the awards are offered to PhD students, with approximately a third of awards available in the US round and the rest in the International round.",
            "The programme was established in October 2000 by an historic donation of US$210m from the Gates Foundation to the University of Cambridge; the first class came into residence in October 2001."
          ],
          "sdg_requirement": false,
          "sdg_requirement_details": [],
          "degree_levels": [
            "PhD",
            "Master's"
