      "status": "open",
      "is_verified": true,
      "last_verified_date": "2026-10-01",
      "description": "The GHES Fellowship is a 12-month, full-time, NIH-supported mentored research training award in global health research, run by a consortium of Yale, Stanford, the University of Arizona and UC Berkeley under the Fogarty International Center's LAUNCH program. It pays a monthly stipend for 12 months on the NIH award stipend scale, plus research support funding (USD 15,000 for postdoctoral fellows, USD 10,000 for predoctoral fellows), visa and passport fees, and - for U.S. applicants - international health insurance, required vaccinations, a reimbursable roundtrip flight to the training site and travel and accommodation for the NIH orientation. Doctorate tuition and fees are expressly not an allowable expense, so this is not full funding for a degree.",
      "program_type": "Government research training fellowship",
      "best_fit": "U.S. citizens or permanent residents, and citizens of the 15 named GHES collaborating LMICs, who hold or are completing a doctoral or health-professional doctorate (PhD, DrPH, MD, DO, DDS, DVM, MBBS, MBChB, MMed, PharmD) within the last five years, can commit 10-12 months in person at a GHES LMIC research site, and can secure a primary GHES U.S. mentor plus a primary GHES LMIC mentor.",
      "funding_amount": null,
      "funding_currency": null,
      "funding_period": null,
      "eligibility_summary": "Open to U.S. predoctoral trainees and to U.S. and LMIC postdoctoral and post-professional trainees whose terminal doctoral or health-professional degree was received within the last five years, who can spend 10-12 months at a GHES LMIC research site and who have both a primary GHES U.S. mentor and a primary GHES LMIC mentor.",
      "eligibility": [
