      ],
      "selection_notes": "A panel of external reviewers reads and scores each application in five areas: success as a GHES fellow; aptitude and interest in research; potential for a career in global health; research proposal; and plan for mentoring and training. The GHES Principal Investigators may conduct online interviews with applicants if deemed necessary. Final selection of fellows is made by the GHES executive committee based on the candidate's application, research proposal, supplemental materials, external reviews and scores. The first round of fellowship awards is likely to be offered in April and the second round likely between May and June. Accepted applicants are notified by email, followed by a formal letter of award; candidates who are not selected are informed by email.",
      "notes": "Not covered: doctorate tuition and fees are explicitly not an allowable GHES expense ('Would a GHES fellowship help pay for my doctorate degree tuition/fees? No, that is not an allowable expense.'). The GHES does not fund LMIC students (student scholars) at LMIC institutions. The GHES LMIC fellowship is not intended to support a stay as a visiting researcher in the US. Award conditions: this is a full-time 12-month fellowship (40 hours per week for all 12 months); trainees will not be permitted to hold another job, even temporary or part-time, during the fellowship. All stipends are potentially taxable under U.S. and international law; GHES cannot provide tax advice and fellows are encouraged to consult a qualified tax professional. Partial fellowships can be provided to applicants already supported - for example T32 fellows can be awarded research-related and travel funds while their stipend is provided by the T32 fellowship - but FIC concurrent support must be allowable from the other source's perspective. The Stanford page notes that one fellow received a seed grant to continue research with the mentor team after the fellowship; this is not a published entitlement of the award.",
      "detail": {
        "official_details": {
          "description": "The Global Health Emerging Scholars (GHES) Fellowship is a 12-month, NIH-supported, mentored training in global health research designed to improve population health. Hosted by a consortium of Yale University, Stanford University, University of Arizona and UC Berkeley, the fellowship year typically runs July-June and offers training opportunities in 15 countries. GHES is part of the Global Health Program for Fellows and Scholars / Launching Future Leaders in Global Health Research Training Program (LAUNCH) sponsored by the Fogarty International Center (FIC) and several collaborating NIH institutes and centers.",
          "purpose": [
            "The program's main objective is to train new generations of health researchers and professionals who will be prepared to address the emerging challenges in global health.",
            "GHES encourages research on health challenges that result from rapid urbanization and social inequities, on challenges of delivering accessible and high-quality healthcare within resource-limited settings, and on interventions that address the efficient management of scarce resources and innovative solutions to improving health services.",
            "Research areas include (but are not limited to) chronic and noncommunicable diseases, HIV/STIs and other infectious diseases, environmental health, maternal and child health, intentional and unintentional injuries, trauma, and mental health.",
            "The program was established in 2012 and is comprised of four U.S. partner institutions - Yale University, the University of California at Berkeley, Stanford University and the University of Arizona - with collaborations in 15 LMICs representing Africa, Latin America, the Caribbean, South and Southeast Asia, the Middle East, Eastern Europe and Oceania."
          ],
          "sdg_requirement": false,
          "sdg_requirement_details": [],
          "degree_levels": [
            "Predoctoral",
            "Postdoctoral",
            "Post-professional"
          ],
          "study_type": [
            "Full-time, in-person research training at an LMIC research site"
          ],
          "degree_award": "Not a degree programme. It is a 12-month research training fellowship; no degree or certificate is awarded.",
          "duration": "12 months, full time (40 hours per week for all 12 months, 100% of the trainee's time). The fellowship year typically runs July to June and training begins 1 July.",
          "application_cycle": "Annual. Applications for the 2027-28 fellowship year are live, with materials due by 1 October 2026. First round of awards likely in April; second round likely between May and June, ahead of a July start.",
          "deadline_central": "2026-10-01",
          "deadline_central_display": "October 1, 2026 at 5:00pm Eastern Time",
          "deadline_institutional": "None published. A single consortium-wide deadline applies across Yale, Stanford, the University of Arizona and UC Berkeley; no institution-level deadline is published.",
          "deadline_note": "The deadline falls on 1 October 2026 at 5:00pm Eastern Time, which is the last_verified_date of this record. The application was therefore open on the day of verification but closes the same day; anyone reading this after 5:00pm Eastern Time on 1 October 2026 cannot apply to this cycle. No later re-opening or next-cycle date is published on the pages read.",
          "status": "OPEN",
          "funding_type": "Stipend",
          "funding_label": "Competitive monthly stipend for 12 months on the NIH award stipend scale, plus research support funding, visa and passport fees, and for U.S. fellows international health insurance, vaccinations, a reimbursable roundtrip flight to the training site and travel and accommodation for the NIH orientation",
          "funding_amount": null,
          "funding_currency": null,
          "funding_period": null,
          "tuition_coverage": "None. Doctorate tuition and fees are explicitly not covered: 'Would a GHES fellowship help pay for my doctorate degree tuition/fees? No, that is not an allowable expense.'",
          "living_cost_coverage": "The monthly stipend is intended to cover living expenses including food, housing and basic transportation. The stipend is described as competitive and determined by the NIH award stipend scale paired with the fellow's paired U.S. institution; no single stipend figure is published, and the amount therefore varies by fellow.",
          "travel_coverage": "U.S. fellows: a reimbursable budget covering a roundtrip flight to the training site, where the amount varies by U.S. home institution, plus travel and accommodation to attend the NIH orientation in July. LMIC postdoctoral fellows: USD 15,000 to support travel and the short-term U.S.-based training period.",
          "fully_funded": false,
          "tuition_rule": "GHES pays no tuition. It is a research training fellowship, not a scholarship, and doctorate tuition and fees are stated to be not an allowable expense.",
          "home_tuition_rule": "Not applicable and not published. Because GHHE pays no tuition at all, there is no published rule about whether home-country or home-institution tuition continues during the award.",
          "host_institution_fee": "Not published. No fee payable to a host institution is described on the pages read; trainees are hosted at GHES-affiliated research sites and universities rather than enrolled as fee-paying students.",
          "tax_status": "All stipends are potentially taxable under U.S. and international law. GHES cannot provide tax advice and fellows are encouraged to consult a qualified tax professional.",
          "covered_expenses": [
            "A monthly stipend for 12 months (competitive, set by the NIH award stipend scale paired with the fellow's paired U.S. institution), covering living expenses including food, housing and basic transportation",
            "Research support funding: USD 15,000 for U.S. postdoctoral fellows",
            "Research support funding: USD 10,000 for U.S. predoctoral fellows",
            "Research support funding: USD 15,000 for LMIC postdoctoral fellows",
            "An additional USD 5,000 available to all fellows to supplement the fellow's project and support the mentorship team",
            "USD 15,000 for LMIC postdoctoral fellows to support travel and the short-term U.S.-based training period",
            "Visa and passport fees",
            "International health insurance for U.S. applicants",
            "Required vaccinations for U.S. applicants",
            "A reimbursable budget for a roundtrip flight to the training site for U.S. applicants (amount varies by U.S. home institution)",
            "Travel and accommodation to attend the orientation at NIH in July"
          ],
          "not_covered_or_excluded": [
            "Doctorate tuition and fees - explicitly 'not an allowable expense'",
            "The GHES does not fund LMIC students (student scholars) at LMIC institutions",
            "The GHES LMIC fellowship is not intended to support a stay as a visiting researcher in the US; it is for doctoral researchers to conduct their project and receive mentorship at their home LMIC institution",
            "Research activities already funded from another source - FIC cannot support research activities already funded, so applicants must show how the GHES proposal differs from their funded activities or covers expenses not supported by the other funding",
            "Any stipend for a fellow who holds other support, unless the programme grants a partial fellowship (for example research-related and travel funds for T32 fellows whose stipend is provided by the T32 award), and only where FIC concurrent support is allowable from the other source's perspective",
            "Applicants who cannot commit 10 to 11 months of living abroad - this is a main programme requirement"
          ],
          "benefits": [
            "Mentored research training at established biomedical and health research sites in low- and middle-income countries",
            "A paired primary U.S. mentor and primary LMIC mentor structure, with decisions on the project and training plan made jointly and approved by both mentors",
            "For LMIC postdoctoral fellows, a required 1-3 month training experience at the primary U.S. mentor's university with structured learning, skills development, networking and mentoring",
            "Access to GHES research training sites across 15 countries",
            "Long-term collaborations with mentors; the Stanford page reports that fellows have built ongoing project collaborations and that at least one fellow received a seed grant to continue research with the mentor team, though this is not a published entitlement"
          ],
          "eligibility": [
            "U.S. Postdoctoral Applicants (PhD, MD, DVM, DrPH, etc.)",
            "U.S. Predoctoral Applicants",
            "LMIC Postdoctoral Applicants"
          ],
          "general_eligibility": [
            "GHES is designed for pre-doctoral trainees from the U.S. and post-doctoral and post-professional trainees from the U.S. and LMICs.",
            "Pre-doctoral trainees must be enrolled in a program leading to a PhD or in an equivalent research doctoral degree program.",
            "Postdoctoral trainees must have received, as of the beginning date of the training period, a Ph.D., M.D., D.D.S., or comparable doctoral degree from an accredited domestic or foreign institution.",
            "Comparable doctoral degrees include, but are not limited to, D.M.D., DC, DO, DVM, OD, DPM, ScD, EngD, DrPH, DNSc, DPT, PharmD, ND, DSW, PsyD, MMed, MBBS, MBChB, as well as a doctoral degree in nursing research.",
            "All applicants must have a primary GHES U.S. mentor and a primary LMIC mentor who are part of the GHES program; additional mentors may be proposed.",
            "U.S. post-doctoral and pre-doctoral applicants can be from any college or institution in the country and do not have to be affiliated with Stanford, Arizona, Berkeley or Yale, but must have a professional relationship with a GHES U.S. mentor at one of those universities.",
            "LMIC post-doctoral applicants must be part of or affiliated with an institution in one of the GHES collaborating LMICs.",
            "Professional relationship means a relationship between individuals in a professional setting in which proposed mentors support the applicant's research focus areas and the applicant is willing to listen and work with them."
          ],
          "ineligibility_conditions": [
            "The terminal doctoral or health-professional degree must have been received within the last five years; 'If you received your doctoral/health professional degree more than five years ago then a master's degree within the last five years, you are not eligible to apply.'",
            "LMIC applicants are not eligible to apply for predoctoral fellowships.",
            "The programme is not accepting master's or undergraduate students at this time.",
            "Non-US citizens are only allowed to apply for the U.S. predoctoral and postdoctoral fellowships if they can provide proof of U.S. residency (green card).",
            "An applicant with no institutional affiliation applying as a postdoctoral fellow is eligible only if the GHES mentor commits to appoint them in a postdoctoral position at the mentor's affiliated institution.",
            "You are not eligible if you cannot commit to 10-11 months of living abroad at the LMIC research training site.",
            "As a master's or undergraduate student at one of the four GHES institutions, you are not eligible to apply.",
            "U.S. applicants who are not affiliated with UC Berkeley, the University of Arizona, Stanford or Yale must receive approval to apply from the GHES program manager and must provide an NIH biosketch and the names of potential GHES mentors already contacted (per the Stanford GHES page).",
            "To apply as an LMIC fellow you must already have, or secure, an affiliation or appointment with one of the GHES research sites.",
            "The GHES does not fund LMIC students at LMIC institutions, so an LMIC student-scholar cannot be funded under the LMIC route."
          ],
          "citizenship_residency": "U.S. applicants must be a citizen of the United States or its territories or possessions, or a U.S. permanent resident; a permanent resident number must be included in the application of a U.S. permanent resident. LMIC applicants must be a citizen of one of the collaborating LMICs: Bangladesh, Burkina Faso, Colombia, Ecuador, Ethiopia, India, Kenya, Lebanon, Malaysia, Nigeria, Peru, Samoa, Senegal, Tanzania, Uganda.",
          "international_eligibility": "International eligibility is limited to postdoctoral applicants who are citizens of the 15 named collaborating LMICs and who are residents of, or affiliated with, a GHES research site in one of those countries. International students enrolled in a PhD programme at a U.S. consortium university who are not U.S. citizens are not eligible unless they hold a green card.",
          "academic_level_rules": {
            "undergraduate": [
              "Not eligible: 'I am a master's (or undergraduate) student from one of the four GHES institutions, am I eligible to apply? No, the program is not accepting master's or undergraduate students at this time.'"
            ],
            "masters": [
              "Not eligible as an applicant: a master's degree alone does not qualify.",
              "A master's degree completed within the last five years does not restore eligibility if the doctoral or health-professional degree was completed more than five years ago."
            ],
            "phd": [
              "U.S. Predoctoral Applicants must be currently enrolled in a doctoral (e.g. PhD, DrPH) or health professional (e.g. MD, DO, DDS, DVM) degree program and must have passed qualifying examination(s) at the time of application.",
              "If your degree will be completed by 1 July of your fellowship year you may apply for a postdoctoral fellowship; if accepted you must provide proof that you received the degree before officially starting.",
              "Postdoctoral Applicants must have a doctoral or health professional degree by the time training begins (1 July) and within five years of completion.",
              "Predoctoral candidates must submit an unofficial transcript.",
              "U.S. predoctoral research support funding is USD 10,000; U.S. postdoctoral research support funding is USD 15,000; LMIC postdoctoral research support funding is USD 15,000 plus USD 15,000 for travel and the short-term U.S.-based training period; an additional USD 5,000 is available in all cases."
            ]
          },
          "host_requirements": [
            "All applicants must have a primary GHES LMIC mentor who is a faculty member at an institution in one of the GHES collaborating LMICs, and the trainee must spend 10-12 months at that LMIC research training site.",
            "U.S. trainees must have a primary GHES U.S. mentor who is a faculty member at Stanford University, the University of Arizona, UC Berkeley or Yale University.",
            "GHES has designated mentor teams across the U.S. and LMIC institutions; in many instances the U.S. and LMIC mentors are already collaborating.",
            "The applicant must contact the affiliated mentors at the chosen research training site and align the research proposal with their research.",
            "The applicant must contact the GHES program manager at the U.S. mentor's institution, who can answer questions, review application requirements and help prepare materials.",
            "The Section 4 Fellowship Details of the application requires the research training site, mentor names, a mentorship plan, a training plan, IRB approvals and a timeline - so the host mentors must be engaged in building the application.",
            "The NIH Biographical Sketch Common Form for both mentors must be generated and certified through SciENcv before submission."
          ],
          "requirements": [
            "Your application must be completed with the input of your proposed mentors.",
            "One primary GHES U.S. mentor and one primary LMES LMIC mentor who are part of the GHES program.",
            "Complete the online Qualtrics application including the Introduction and Sections 1 to 5.",
            "Submit a research proposal following the published guidelines.",
            "Submit a minimum of 2 and a maximum of 3 letters of recommendation, one of which must be from the primary U.S. GHES mentor and one from the primary LMIC GHES mentor.",
            "Submit an NIH biosketch for the applicant and NIH biosketches for both mentors, all on the NIH Biographical Sketch Common Form certified through SciENcv.",
            "Commit to a full-time 12-month fellowship with no other job.",
            "Attend the mandatory 5-day NIH orientation in Bethesda in mid-July.",
            "U.S. trainees must spend 10-12 months at their LMIC research training site.",
            "LMIC trainees must complete a 1-3 month training experience at their primary U.S. mentor's university.",
            "Disclose previous funding in Section 1 of the application and explain how the GHES proposal differs from already-funded activities."
          ],
          "documents": [
            "Research proposal, following the published GHES research proposal guidelines hosted on the Yale SharePoint; 'Proposals missing any information or formatted incorrectly will not be reviewed.'",
            "Letters of recommendation: minimum 2, maximum 3. One letter must be from the GHES primary U.S. mentor at one of the four participating universities (University of Arizona, University of California Berkeley, Stanford University, Yale University). One letter must be from the GHES primary LMIC mentor at the collaborating LMIC research training site. Additional letters are permitted up to the maximum of three.",
            "Each recommendation letter must include: the mentor's name, position and institution; the mentor's email address; the mentor's phone number; and the mentor's work address. Letters must be addressed to the Global Health Emerging Scholars Fellowship Selection Committee and must be signed by the mentor.",
            "NIH biosketch for the applicant, using the NIH Biographical Sketch Common Form, completed and certified through SciENcv. As of 25 January 2026 NIH no longer uses a separate fellowship biosketch format - all applicants must use the Common Form. An ORCID ID linked to an eRA Commons profile is now required.",
            "NIH biosketches for the U.S. and LMIC mentors, using the NIH Biographical Sketch Common Form, completed and certified through SciENcv; as of 25 January 2026 all biosketches regardless of application type use the Common Form.",
            "Unofficial transcript - required from predoctoral candidates only (Section 1 of the application).",
            "Section 3 requires information on foreign language skills and academic and professional background.",
            "Section 4 requires the research training site, research experience, publications and abstracts, career goals, mentor names, a mentorship plan, a training plan, IRB approvals and a timeline."
          ],
          "document_format_rules": [
            "Recommendation letters must be on the mentor's institutional letterhead.",
            "Recommendation letters must include the mentor's name, position and institution, email address, phone number and work address.",
            "Recommendation letters must be addressed to the Global Health Emerging Scholars Fellowship Selection Committee.",
            "Recommendation letters must be signed by the mentor.",
            "Recommendation letters 'should be scanned or saved as a PDF file and uploaded in the application'.",
            "The research proposal must follow the published GHES research proposal guidelines; proposals missing any information or formatted incorrectly will not be reviewed.",
            "NIH biosketches must use the NIH Biographical Sketch Common Form and must be certified through SciENcv; the older separate fellowship biosketch format is no longer accepted as of 25 January 2026.",
            "An ORCID ID linked to an eRA Commons profile is now required for the applicant's NIH biosketch.",
            "The online application system saves a partially completed application using your computer's IP address, so you can return to it later.",
            "The instruction 'Please avoid submitting multiple applications' applies to the Qualtrics submission.",
            "Predoctoral candidates submit an unofficial transcript; no official or certified transcript requirement is published for this cycle.",
            "No file size limit, accepted file type list beyond the stated PDF for letters, or certified-translation rule is published on the pages read."
          ],
          "selection_criteria": [
            "Success as a GHES fellow",
            "Aptitude and interest in research",
            "Potential for a career in global health",
            "Research proposal",
            "Plan for mentoring and training"
          ],
          "selection_notes": "A panel of external reviewers reads and scores each application in the five published areas. The GHES Principal Investigators may conduct online interviews with applicants if deemed necessary. Final selection of fellows is made by the GHES executive committee and is based on the candidate's application, research proposal, supplemental materials, external reviews and scores. The first round of fellowship awards is likely to be offered in April and the second round likely between May and June. Accepted applicants are notified by email, followed by a formal letter of award; candidates who are not selected are informed by email. On the Stanford page, applicants not affiliated with one of the four consortium universities are directed to send their CV, a short description of their research interests, and the site and faculty member(s) most aligned with their interests to the GHES program manager to verify eligibility before applying.",
          "application_method": [
            "Online application via a Qualtrics form hosted on Yale's survey domain. The application is completed and submitted by the trainee.",
            "The online application system saves a partially completed application using your computer's IP address and you can return to it later.",
            "Mentors upload nothing themselves: the trainee must upload the mentors' letters and NIH biosketches into the application."
          ],
          "application_steps": [
            "Review the programme eligibility criteria to confirm you are eligible.",
            "Review the application requirements (the linked application review document) to familiarise yourself with the application questions and the materials you must submit.",
            "Choose your research area within GHES's encouraged topics.",
            "Choose your desired research training site using the GHES research training sites list, and contact the affiliated mentors there.",
            "Secure a letter of support from one U.S.-based mentor affiliated with the University of Arizona, the University of California at Berkeley, Stanford University or Yale University.",
            "Secure a letter of support from one LMIC mentor affiliated with a research training site in one of the collaborating LMICs.",
            "Contact the GHES program manager at your U.S. mentor's institution so they know about your application and can review requirements and help prepare materials.",
            "Complete the online Qualtrics application: Introduction; Section 1 Eligibility (type of application, institutional affiliation, previous funding; predoctoral candidates also submit an unofficial transcript); Section 2 Personal and Contact Information; Section 3 Professional Background (foreign language skills, academic and professional background); Section 4 Fellowship Details (research training site, research experience, publications/abstracts, career goals, mentor names, mentorship plan, training plan, IRB approvals, timeline); Section 5 Research Proposal and Additional Supplemental Materials.",
            "Prepare the research proposal using the published GHES guidelines - proposals missing information or formatted incorrectly will not be reviewed.",
            "Obtain 2-3 recommendation letters on institutional letterhead, addressed to the Global Health Emerging Scholars Fellowship Selection Committee, signed by the mentor and containing the mentor's name, position, institution, email, phone and work address; scan or save each as a PDF.",
            "Generate the applicant's NIH biosketch on the NIH Biographical Sketch Common Form in SciENcv, with an ORCID ID linked to an eRA Commons profile, and have a mentor review it if this is your first one.",
            "Generate the NIH biosketches for both the U.S. and LMIC mentors on the Common Form in SciENcv.",
            "Upload all documents into the Qualtrics application and submit once by 1 October 2026 at 5:00pm Eastern Time."
          ],
          "applicant_submission_route": [
            "The applicant submits the application personally through the Qualtrics form; mentors do not submit independently.",
            "Mentors supply signed letters and certified NIH biosketches which the applicant uploads.",
            "The GHES program manager at the applicant's U.S. mentor's institution is the named internal contact for the application."
          ],
          "application_portal": "https://yalesurvey.ca1.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_4IUE1rXzjVTibiu",
          "student_direct_application": true,
          "award_holder_conditions": [
            "This is a full-time 12-month fellowship; trainees must devote 100% of their time and will not be permitted to hold another job, even if temporary or part-time, during the fellowship period.",
            "All U.S. trainees must spend 10-12 months at their LMIC research training site.",
            "LMIC trainees spend the majority of the fellowship at their GHES-affiliated LMIC research training site and are required to participate in a 1-3 month training experience at their primary U.S. mentor's university.",
            "All trainees must attend a mandatory 5-day orientation at the National Institutes of Health in Bethesda, Maryland, anticipated to be held mid-July.",
            "Trainees are expected to work closely with their U.S. and LMIC mentors for the duration of the fellowship; mentors provide supervision and oversight and decisions should be made together.",
            "Any significant changes to the research project or training plan must be discussed and approved by both mentors before implementation.",
            "All stipends are potentially taxable under U.S. and international law; fellows should consult a qualified tax professional.",
            "FIC cannot support research activities already funded from another source, so the research proposal must show the difference from existing funding."
          ],
          "deferrable": null,
          "renewable": null,
          "return_home_required": null,
          "clinical_restriction": null,
          "arrival_rules": [
