      "selection_notes": "The programme publishes the priority areas and eligibility priorities rather than a scoring rubric: targeted funding is expected for proposals on HIV, mental health and neurological disorders; applicants from the four consortium universities may be given priority over other U.S. applicants; and doctoral candidates who are in later stages of training (for example following comprehensive examinations or after clinical rotations) will be given priority. The application guidance requires materials to be reviewed by mentors before submission and requires at least two mentors. No interview process, panel description or award-announcement date is published on the pages read.",
      "notes": "Two award types are published, and not every trainee receives a stipend: the Travel and research award deliberately excludes the monthly stipend, so applicants should state or check which award they are applying for. Research funding is capped (up to USD 10,000 postdoctoral, up to USD 7,000 for U.S. doctoral trainees) rather than guaranteed. No stipend amount is published on the pages read. Applicants from the four consortium universities may be given priority, and doctoral candidates must be able to defer studies for the whole fellowship. There is no published deferral, renewal or return-home provision, so those fields are left null. International postdoctoral fellows have a mandatory 1-3 month U.S. attachment that must be planned into the research timeline. The pages publish 11 core research-site countries but state that international trainees must be citizens of 'one of 16 countries where there are program affiliated research institutions'; both statements are recorded and the explicit country list is used.",
