          ],
          "submission_workflow": [
            "Create a FES portal account and complete the online application form for the study scholarship",
            "Upload the supporting documents and the expert opinion",
            "Submit the application before the deadline for your degree stage",
            "Prepare for and attend the first selection interview",
            "Prepare for and attend the second selection interview",
            "Await the decision of the selection committee"
          ],
          "referee_tracking": [
            {
              "role": "Expert/attester (Gutachten)",
              "required": true,
              "instructions": "An expert opinion is submitted together with the application documents; request it early enough to upload it with the application."
            }
          ],
          "post_award_requirements": [
            "Take part in the socio-political seminars, workshops and skills training that form part of the ideelle FÃ¶rderung",
            "The funding is reviewed along with the funding period of the degree programme"
          ],
          "important_advice_for_students": [
            "You must already be enrolled at a German university when you apply. This is the first gate, and it is not waivable — an offer of admission is not enough, enrolment is. Plan around being in Germany before the window closes.",
            "German is mandatory, not optional. B2 is the level the Foundation states it accepts, and it accepts it without a certificate, but the application must still be written and submitted in German. Even if you study in English, you must prove German.",
            "Check your degree-stage window before anything else. The windows are different for Master's, Bachelor, and medicine, and they are expressed relative to your semester count rather than as a calendar date. Applying in the wrong window is the most likely reason for an automatic rejection.",
            "Check the funding-exclusion list against your course. A two-semester Master's, medical study up to the Physikum, any doctoral or postdoc stage, a research visit, physiotherapy or psychotherapy training, part-time study, and study outside the EU are all excluded. If your programme is short or part-time, this is the wrong scheme.",
            "A second degree or a language course is not funded unless you already hold an FES scholarship. If you are planning to change course, check whether the new course would be a second degree.",
            "Have your first graded performance records in hand before you apply. They are a stated prerequisite, and Bachelor's students must submit them.",
            "Do the two academic references early. You have three weeks for documents but up to six weeks for the references, so the references are the long pole. University lecturers, not teachers, write them unless you are a first-year student.",
            "Write the civic engagement part seriously. Soci-political commitment and identifying with the values of social democracy are two of the three baseline yes/no questions, and the Foundation looks for students who identify with social democracy and treat volunteering as self-evident.",
            "Play to the Foundation's published priority groups if any apply to you: first in your family to study, migration background, women, universities of applied sciences, STEM. The Foundation states it gives particular weight to these applications.",
            "Budget for what is not covered. The published package is 992 Euro per month plus health insurance costs, with an additional amount for children. Tuition and semester fees at your institution are not listed as covered. Ask your university what the semester contribution is and keep it in your own budget.",
            "Watch the offset rule. The Foundation's English page states that any income exceeding 400 Euro per month is credited against the scholarship, so part-time earnings above that threshold reduce your stipend.",
            "Claim interview travel costs if you have them. The Foundation reimburses travel costs for applicants invited to a selection interview, using its own form. Submit it.",
            "Allow three to seven months. The process runs from the online form through a document request, two interviews with two separate Gutachten, and a committee meeting, and the committee only sits eight to ten times a year.",
            "Talk to a scholarship ambassador before you apply. The Foundation points applicants to its stipendien-botschafter:innen for questions and for encouragement before applying, and these are former holders who answer questions they would rather answer themselves."
          ],
          "questions_to_ask_host": [
            "What is the current semester contribution fee at my institution, and does the university offer any waiver for scholarship holders? The Foundation does not publish that it pays it.",
            "What health insurance plan will I be enrolled in, and does its cost vary with my age, marital status or dependants? The Foundation says it covers health insurance costs but publishes no figure.",
            "What counts as the 'first graded performance records' required at application, and does my university issue them in a form I can upload directly?",
            "Does my university offer a thesis supervisor or academic mentor willing to write a Gutachten, and how long do they take?",
            "What is the standard period of study for my programme, so that I can calculate the three-semester application window correctly?",
            "Is there a German course requirement attached to my programme that I should start now, given that applications must be submitted in German?",
            "Does my institution have a scholarship-holder network or working group that FES scholarship holders join, and what does it expect of me in terms of time?",
            "How much do the 18 thematic working groups and the political education seminars take in study time, on top of my coursework?",
            "Is my course one of the excluded categories by another name — for example is it run part-time, is it a two-semester Master's, or is it a research stay rather than a degree programme?",
            "What is the Foundation's offset calculation in practice if I have part-time income, and over what period is it assessed?",
            "How do I apply for the additional amount for scholarship holders with children, and what is the current monthly figure?",
            "Can I change my host institution or my subject after being awarded, and does that affect the stipend?"
          ]
        },
        "verification": {
          "cycle_verified": "Rolling annual cycle for the Grundförderung für Ausländer:innen, with degree-stage windows rather than a single annual deadline: Master's by 30 November (winter semester) or 31 May (summer semester); Bachelor, Diplom, Magister and Staatsexamen after the first semester and up to three semesters before the end of the standard period of study; medicine after the fourth semester or Physikum to the end of the sixth semester. Selection committee meets eight to ten times a year; total process three to seven months. The record's status open reflects that the programme is published as continuously accepting applications within these windows.",
          "verification_confidence": 0.93,
          "official_fields_confirmed": [
            "Programme scope: Grundförderung für Ausländer:innen includes Bachelor's, Master's and Diplom degree programmes and programmes leading to the Staatsexamen (Grundförderung page)",
            "Applications formally possible both from Germany and from abroad, and the Foundation is particularly pleased to enable students from abroad to study at a German university",
            "Prerequisites: first graded performance records and good German language skills; B2 accepted, also without a certificate; German must be proved even if the applicant studies in English",
            "The six published suitability criteria: foreign national; already studying at a German public or state-recognised university or university of applied sciences; very good German; above-average grades; socio-political engagement; identification with the values of social democracy",
            "Stated purpose, both material and ideological, and the stated aim that holders can concentrate on their studies and have more time for their engagement",
            "Ideological support: political education seminars, workshops and skills training, local scholarship-holder groups in numerous cities, 18 thematic working groups, integration into the network of current and former holders, and personal Betreuung und Beratung with former holders acting as Mentor:innen",
            "Material package on the programme page: 992 Euro financial support, health insurance costs covered, and an additional 255 Euro per month for scholarship holders with children, plus the explicit statement that the scholarship does not have to be repaid",
            "Stage-specific application windows for Master's, Bachelor, Diplom, Magister, Staatsexamen and medicine, with the calendar dates 30 November and 31 May for the Master's winter and summer semesters",
            "Education-equity weighting: particular consideration for first-generation students and/or students with a migration background, women, students at universities of applied sciences, and STEM students",
            "The three baseline questions an applicant must answer with a clear yes, and the instruction to apply as early as possible",
            "The full five-step selection procedure with the two-to-four-week first outcome, three weeks for documents and up to six weeks for references, two selection interviews producing two Gutachten, and the selection committee deciding at a meeting held eight to ten times a year, with notification at the latest one week after the meeting",
            "The full second-stage document list: detailed CV with education and socio-political engagement, certificates, university entrance qualification, enrolment certificate and other evidence, detailed letter of motivation, and two Gutachten from university lecturers or teachers for first-year students",
            "Applications must be submitted in German, and living in Germany and studying at a German university is essential",
            "The extensive published exclusion list: psychotherapist training, short-term stays abroad, project proposals, courses of under three semesters including two-semester Master's programmes, research visits, trainee programmes, university preparatory courses, medical studies up to and including the Physikum, doctoral degrees and further medical education and training, physiotherapy training, final stages of a degree or doctorate, postdoctoral projects, study abroad outside the EU, and extra-occupational or part-time study; plus language courses and second degrees unless the applicant already holds an FES scholarship",
            "The offset rule on the Foundation's English page: any income exceeding 400 Euro per month will be credited against the scholarship",
            "The Foundation's English page states 992 € per month for students and 1.500 € per month for postgraduates, and 276 € of family allowance if applicable; these are recorded as a source conflict with the programme page's 255 € figure for children",
            "Travel-cost reimbursement for applicants invited to a selection interview, with a dedicated Grundförderung form published",
            "The exact application form URL for the Grundförderung für Ausländer:innen and the applicant login URL",
            "The published contact block for Auslandsförderung: Elisabeth Schulze Horn (phone 0228 883-7932) for surnames A to N and Rebecca Schmitz (phone +49 228 883-7967) for surnames O to Z, plus the dedicated Grundförderung document checklist file and the three-step reference guidance",
            "The published Basisförderung route for doctoral candidates is a separate programme, Promotionsförderung für Ausländer:innen, which funds all dissertation projects except medicine"
          ],
          "institution_check_required": [
            "host_institution_fee left null: no official page states whether the German institution's tuition or semester contribution fees are payable or waived; the Foundation's package description covers the stipend and health insurance only",
            "tax_status left null: no official page states the tax treatment of the 992 Euro stipend in Germany or whether it is treated as taxable income",
            "deferrable left null: no official page states whether an award can be deferred, postponed or started in a later semester",
            "renewable left null: no official page states whether the scholarship can be renewed, extended or re-applied for in a later year, although the stage-based windows and the second-degree exclusion imply a per-degree structure",
            "return_home_required left null: no official page states any obligation to return to the applicant's country of origin or to practise there after graduation; no bond is published",
            "SOURCE CONFLICT on the children's allowance: the Grundförderung page states 'Stipendiat:innen mit Kind erhalten zusätzlich 255 Euro im Monat', while the Foundation's English 'Information in English' page states '276 € of family allowance, if applicable'. Both figures are recorded and the programme-specific German page figure of 255 Euro is treated as governing for this programme. Confirm with the Foundation before budgeting.",
            "SOURCE CONFLICT on the postgraduate rate: the English page states 1.500 € per month for postgraduates, which is a different programme (Promotionsförderung) rather than the Grundförderung; the Grundförderung page gives a single figure of 992 Euro with no postgraduate differential",
            "SOURCE CONFLICT on the Bachelor/Diplom deadline: the English page states 'diploma / magister / state examination: by the end of 6th semester' and separately 'Bachelor/undergraduate programmes: until 3 semesters before finishing the standard period of study', while the German programme page states one combined window for Bachelor, Diplom, Magister and Staatsexamen of after the first semester up to three semesters before the end of the standard period of study. Both are recorded; the German programme page governs",
            "clinical_restriction recorded as true on a narrow basis: the Foundation explicitly does not fund medical studies up to and including the Physikum, doctoral degrees and further education or training in the medical field, and states that the separate doctoral programme funds all dissertation projects except medicine. This is a funding exclusion rather than a clinical-practice restriction on an award holder, and no health, fitness or medical-clearance condition on continuing students is published",
            "arrival_rules could not be completed with a date: because the applicant must already be enrolled in Germany when applying, no arrival provision is published, and no visa, relocation or travel-to-Germany funding is stated",
            "The Grundförderung document and reference checklist file at https://www.fes.de/index.php?eID=dumpFile&t=f&f=160624 was identified as the programme's governing document list but was not downloaded, so file formats, size limits, translation rules, certification rules and any page limits within it are unconfirmed",
            "The reference-writing guidance, the Merkblatt und Studienverlaufsplan file (f=165879), and the travel-cost reimbursement form (f=165947) were identified but not read",
            "The online application form itself was not opened, so any upload format, file size or character limit enforced by the form is unconfirmed",
            "No official page states how the 992 Euro is affected by a part-time workload in practice, only the English page's statement that income above 400 € per month is credited against the scholarship",
            "No official page states whether the applicant may change institution, subject or degree stage after being awarded",
            "No official page states an appeals process against a rejection at any of the five stages"
          ],
          "unverified_or_removed_claims": [
            "Rejected the claim that the scholarship covers tuition or semester fees: no official page lists them, so tuition_coverage is not asserted and host_institution_fee is null",
            "Rejected any English-language application route: the Foundation states applications have to be submitted in German and that German language skills are absolutely necessary",
            "Rejected any claim that a German certificate must be produced: the Foundation states it accepts a level of B2, also without a certificate, so english_requirement remains null and the German requirement is recorded instead",
            "Rejected any claim of return-home obligation or service bond: none is published",
            "Rejected a specific interview date: the Foundation publishes only that the selection committee meets eight to ten times a year, so no date was invented",
            "Demoted the English page's 1.500 € postgraduate figure as a different programme (doctoral funding), and recorded the conflict rather than merging the two rates",
            "Demoted the English page's 276 € family allowance in favour of the programme-specific 255 € figure, recording the conflict explicitly",
            "Rejected any SDG or development-goal requirement: the Foundation's test is identification with the values of social democracy, so sdg_requirement is false"
          ],
          "source_conflict_rule": "The programme page for Grundförderung für Ausländer:innen governs this specific scheme's scope, package and degree-stage windows, and the online application page governs the procedure, document list and deadlines. The Foundation's English 'Information in English' page is a cross-programme summary covering all FES schemes; where it differs from the programme page, the programme-specific German page governs, and every difference is recorded in institution_check_required rather than silently resolved. Where the English page is the only source for a rule, such as the 400 € income offset, it is cited explicitly as the English page.",
          "source_freshness_rule": "The cycle is continuous and the windows are semester-relative rather than calendar-anchored, so there is no single annual date to go stale; what does change is the stipend rate, the family or children's allowance, the fee schedule at German institutions and the exclusion list. All of these must be re-read each academic cycle before an applicant budgets, and the selection committee's eight-to-ten-meetings-per-year cadence means a rejection is not final until a specific meeting date has passed.",
          "source_citations": [
            "https://www.fes.de/studienfoerderung/stipendium/grundfoerderung-fuer-auslaender-innen",
            "https://www.fes.de/studienfoerderung/bewerbung",
            "https://www.fes.de/studienfoerderung/information-in-english",
            "https://www.fes.de/oas/portal/pls/portal/bewsti2014.bewmaske?pFprog=2&pkn=A"
          ],
          "researched_by": "deep_01",
          "last_verified_date": "2026-10-01",
          "data_quality": {
            "source_level": "Official provider pages (Friedrich-Ebert-Stiftung programme page for the Grundförderung, the Foundation's online application page, and the Foundation's own English-language information page), all on fes.de",
            "coverage": "High for scope, eligibility, exclusions, package and procedure: the programme page carries the six suitability criteria, the German B2 rule, the full material package with the non-repayment statement and the children's amount, the ideological support offering and the degree-stage windows with calendar dates; the application page carries the five-step procedure, both interview stages, the two-to-four-week and three-week and six-week response windows, the full document list, the committee cadence and the two named contacts. Gaps are confined to format rules inside a separate checklist PDF, the tax treatment, fee questions, and three genuine conflicts between the German programme page and the English summary page.",
            "pages_read": [
              "https://www.fes.de/studienfoerderung/stipendium/grundfoerderung-fuer-auslaender-innen",
              "https://www.fes.de/studienfoerderung/bewerbung",
              "https://www.fes.de/studienfoerderung/information-in-english"
            ],
            "pages_unreadable": [
              "https://www.fes.de/index.php?eID=dumpFile&t=f&f=160624 - the Foundation's Grundförderung document and reference checklist, which the Foundation states is the governing list of required documents and further instructions for this application step; identified but not downloaded, so document format, size, translation and certification rules remain unconfirmed",
              "https://www.fes.de/index.php?eID=dumpFile&t=f&f=165879 - Merkblatt und Studienverlaufsplan (guidance sheet and study progression plan); identified but not read",
              "https://www.fes.de/index.php?eID=dumpFile&t=f&f=165947 - the travel-cost reimbursement form for the Grundförderung; identified but not read",
              "https://www.fes.de/oas/portal/pls/portal/bewsti2014.bewmaske?pFprog=2&pkn=A - the online application form itself; identified from the application page but not opened, so any technical upload or format limits enforced by the form are unconfirmed"
            ]
          }
        }
      },
