        "applicant_utility": {
          "application_readiness_checklist": [
            "Confirm your nationality is on the eligible-country list and that you hold no Korean citizenship, including dual citizenship",
            "Confirm you hold or expect a bachelor's degree",
            "Apply to one of the six designated Korean graduate schools and secure admission",
            "Alert your admitting department that the graduate school must submit a recommendation to the Foundation, and ask when they will do so",
            "Confirm your intended major sits within the humanities, social sciences, or natural sciences/engineering grouping",
            "Prepare for a Foundation interview around January",
            "Ask the admitting university for its own deadline for submitting the recommendation, since the Foundation publishes only 'around December'"
          ],
          "important_advice_for_students": [
            "Start with university admission, not with the scholarship. Admission to a designated graduate school is stage 01 of the Foundation's own five-stage procedure, so an application strategy that starts with the Foundation cannot work.",
            "The single most common failure point is forgetting the recommendation. The applicant cannot submit it; the graduate school does. Ask the department, in writing, when it will send the recommendation to the Foundation, and check back in November rather than assuming.",
            "Treat the eligible-country and eligible-university lists as provisional. The Foundation marks both as tentative and separately marks the 20-student quota as subject to change, so confirm the current year's lists with asiafellowship@postf.org before you commit to a university.",
            "Do not plan to arrive in January. Every Foundation stage date is published with a tilde, from around November to around February, and the only fixed date in the cycle is the March certificate award ceremony.",
            "Budget from the caps, not the headline. Tuition is full only for 4 semesters (Master's) or 6 semesters (doctoral or integrated), and living expenses only for 22 or 36 months. If your programme runs longer, the extra time is your own cost.",
            "Insurance is handled: the National Health Insurance premium is a published benefit. Airfare is not on the published benefit list, so fund your own travel.",
            "Both eligible-country and eligible-university lists are published as tentative on the official site, so do not rely on a list you read from a secondary source."
          ],
          "common_rejection_risks": [
            "Holding Korean citizenship, including as a dual citizen",
            "Applying to a university that is not on the designated list",
            "Coming from a country not on the eligible-country list",
            "Missing the graduate school's recommendation step, since the applicant cannot apply directly",
            "Not being admitted before the recommendation is due, because admission is the gateway stage",
            "Applying for a major outside the humanities, social sciences, or natural sciences/engineering grouping",
            "Missing the Foundation interview, published as around January",
            "Assuming the scholarship covers the whole programme when tuition and living expenses are capped by semester and month count"
          ],
          "estimated_uncovered_costs": [
