      "selection_notes": "Applicants are evaluated on academic excellence, technical preparation, research potential, and alignment with their chosen program. Admission to MBZUAI is highly competitive with limited places available in each incoming cohort; MBZUAI states that forty-three percent of its incoming graduate students for the 2026-2027 academic year came from the world's top computer science universities. Funding decisions for M.Sc. students depend on whether a faculty advisor sponsors a research or teaching assistantship or, for UAE nationals, an approved government scholarship, with merit-based funding considered during the admissions review process.",
      "notes": "M.Sc. students are fee-paying by default and funding is NOT guaranteed for all admitted students; students without a funding source are classified as fee-paying. Ph.D. funding is subject to a confirmed advisor match and satisfactory progress, and transitions to the faculty advisor's funding no later than the start of Year 2. Funded students are expected to maintain an in-person presence on campus throughout their funded period and to obtain approval before being away. Students receiving external scholarships or fellowships must disclose this funding to the University; external funding normally replaces the University stipend rather than being paid in addition to it. The Master in Applied Artificial Intelligence (MAAI) is fee-based at AED 5,000 per credit, 34 credit hours, total tuition AED 170,000 (approx. USD 46,291), and is not eligible for University or faculty-sponsored funding. Cost of Attendance figures for Fall 2027 for unfunded M.Sc. students are currently being finalized and were not published on the page at the time of verification. An English language waiver exists and is described on the MBZUAI admissions FAQ, but the minimum score thresholds are published only on programme-specific pages, so no score is recorded here.",
