          "program_type": "University-funded graduate scholarship and assistantship (Mohammed bin Zayed University of Artificial Intelligence)",
          "region": "Middle East (United Arab Emirates, Abu Dhabi); open to UAE nationals and international students",
          "coverage": "Covers free tuition fees, a monthly stipend, one return airfare each year to the home country, health insurance for the approved funding period, and a University-sponsored UAE residence visa and Emirates ID (where eligible). Excludes: external scholarship income (which replaces the University stipend rather than adding to it), the fee-based Master in Applied Artificial Intelligence, and any M.Sc. student without a secured funding source, who is classified as fee-paying. The published AED table is unlabelled as to period.",
          "english_requirement": "All applicants whose first language is not English must demonstrate proficiency through one of the accepted exams at the published minimum overall score: IELTS Academic 6.5; TOEFL iBT 90/4.5; Duolingo English Test 120. Exams must be administered at an approved physical test centre and Home Edition exams are not accepted; Duolingo results are only accepted when IELTS/TOEFL/PTE/Cambridge access is limited and evidence of limited access may be requested. Results are valid for 2 years from the test date.",
          "english_requirement_note": "Full exemption: a degree entirely taught and assessed in English at a university in a country where English is both an official language and the dominant language of instruction in higher education, including American Samoa, Australia, Botswana, Canada (excluding Quebec), Fiji, Ghana, Guyana, Ireland, Jamaica, Kenya, Lesotho, Liberia, New Zealand, Nigeria, Papua New Guinea, Samoa, Singapore, Solomon Islands, South Africa, Tonga, Trinidad and Tobago, United Kingdom, United States, Zambia, Zimbabwe. Conditional exemption: a degree completed elsewhere may still qualify with official documentation on university letterhead signed by an academic official confirming the entire programme was taught and assessed in English. A Medium of Instruction letter is provisionally accepted at submission, subject to review and verification; if not approved, an approved ELP test score must be submitted within the deadline set by Admissions. The application is considered incomplete if the English proficiency component is not included by the deadline.",
