        "applicant_utility": {
          "application_readiness_checklist": [
            "Check the PhD timing rule first: you must hold a doctorate or equivalent at the start of the fellowship, and if you already held the PhD when you applied it must have been obtained within the previous two years.",
            "Confirm you have at least one first author paper accepted or published in an international peer reviewed journal. If not, EMBO accepts a first author preprint with public in-depth peer reviews from an independent peer review service; individual comments, highlights and informal reviews do not count.",
            "Test your host against every mobility exclusion: different country from your PhD country, not a lab where you worked more than six months, not your PhD lab, institute or department, and not your previous supervisor's lab anywhere.",
            "If you have already moved to the host country, confirm you have been living there for less than six months at the time you submit.",
            "Choose a project that is not a direct continuation of your PhD work.",
            "Plan for one application only, at one host laboratory, for one project, in a given round. You may reapply once, and only if your second application is submitted before 22 January 2027 at 14:00 CET - after that, reapplication is not permitted.",
            "Contact the EMBO Fellowship Office before submitting if you rely on any exception, such as parental leave, severe illness or military service. There are no exceptions for voluntary career breaks, and an application outside the criteria without prior approval is deemed ineligible.",
            "Secure two references and the receiving institute's acceptance, and submit them through the online system, not by email.",
            "Submit everything early enough to confirm completion before 14:00 CET on the cut-off date. Registering an account is not the same as completing an application, and anything confirmed after 14:00 CET on the day goes to the next round.",
            "Plan your start date within one calendar year of the relevant cut-off, and decide now whether you might request part-time working or parental leave, since both have published request routes."
          ],
          "important_advice_for_students": [
            "This is a postdoctoral award, not a PhD scholarship. If you do not yet hold a doctorate, you are not eligible.",
            "The mobility rules are the scheme's purpose, not a formality. A host in your own country, your old lab, your PhD department, or your previous supervisor's lab will disqualify the application outright, and the Fellowship Office must approve exceptions in advance.",
            "Read the award correctly depending on where you go. In EMBC Member States you are employed by the host institution and EMBO funds the contract; elsewhere you receive a stipend that EMBO says is solely intended to cover the cost of living in the host country. The rates differ, and neither is published on the pages read - both are in the Contract rates sheet.",
            "Do not expect the host to gain anything. EMBO states the fellowship does not cover bench fees, overheads, consumables or any other financial benefit to the host supervisor or institute, so ask the host how it will cover your research costs.",
            "Plan your childcare and family benefits early. In EMBC countries you are in the host's welfare system, including child benefits; outside EMBC countries you get a prorated daycare allowance of up to 2,500 euros per year per child under six, and an allowance for dependent children under 18 whose value depends on the host country.",
            "Claim the Laboratory Leadership Course, but do not assume a place. Waivers are limited each year and allocated first come, first served, and are available only within three years of the award and only if the fellowship runs at least six months.",
            "If you have a child during the fellowship, the extension routes are different by host type. Non-EMBC fellows get three months paid parental leave plus an equal extension; EMBC-based fellows get an extension at no cost to EMBO if the leave is covered by the host country's welfare system and/or the hosting institute. In both cases the host supervisor must request it and you must tell the Fellowship Office.",
            "The two-year default is not a guarantee of two years of your choosing. Shorter durations can be requested down to one year, and the maximum total duration including any part-time period is 36 months."
          ],
          "common_rejection_risks": [
            "Not holding a doctorate or equivalent at the start of the fellowship.",
            "Having obtained the PhD more than two years before submitting the application.",
            "Having no first author peer-reviewed publication, and relying on an informal preprint review, an individual's comment, or a highlight - all explicitly excluded.",
            "A host in the country where the PhD was obtained.",
            "A host laboratory where the candidate previously worked for more than six months, even across several visits.",
            "The candidate's own PhD laboratory, institute or department, or a laboratory of their previous supervisor anywhere.",
            "Having lived in the host country for six months or more at the time of application.",
            "Proposing a project that continues the PhD work directly.",
            "Submitting more than one application, at more than one host laboratory, in the same round.",
            "Missing the 14:00 CET cut-off, because applications confirmed after that time on deadline day enter the next round with no exceptions.",
            "Registering an account but not confirming completion of the application, which does not enter the round.",
            "Relying on an eligibility exception without prior approval from the Fellowship Office - the application is deemed ineligible.",
            "Reapplying after 22 January 2027 at 14:00 CET, when reapplication is no longer permitted."
