      "detail": {
        "official_details": {
          "description": "The Royal Society's Career Development Fellowship is a four-year, postdoctoral research fellowship for the most talented early career scientists from underrepresented groups in STEM, designed to support their retention in STEM and their first step towards establishing research in a UK institution. Applicants can request up to Â£703,800 over four years, covering a contribution to the fellow's salary and on-costs at 80%, directly allocated costs at 80% and research expenses at 100%, with relocation and visa costs for the applicant and dependants claimable in addition to that cap. The scheme is currently a pilot restricted to researchers from Black heritage backgrounds including mixed Black heritage, and it is funded by the Department for Business, Innovation, Science and Trade together with Royal Society charitable funds, with a parallel Alzheimerâ€™s Society-funded variant for dementia research.",
          "purpose": [
            "This scheme provides the most talented early career scientists from underrepresented groups in STEM with research funding and high-quality training opportunities to build a strong base for a successful research career.",
            "The Career Development Fellowship (CDF) is a four-year, postdoctoral research fellowship that aims to support the retention in STEM of researchers from underrepresented backgrounds.",
            "This fellowship aims to: support talented early career researchers from underrepresented backgrounds in taking the first step towards establishing their research in a UK institution; provide researchers with the opportunity to undertake high-quality curiosity-led research; provide the most promising researchers with the mentoring, networking opportunities, training and support required to establish a successful research career in the UK; and support talented researchers from underrepresented groups to pursue a career in STEM and strengthen the UK scientific workforce.",
            "The scheme was developed following cross-sector roundtables and Royal Society-commissioned reports that looked at trends across 11 years of Higher Education Statistics Authority (HESA) data and benchmarked the Society's early career fellowships against the eligible applicant pool. This identified a need for interventions across the academic pipeline to support researchers from Black or Mixed Black African, Black Caribbean or other Black heritage backgrounds, particularly researchers making the transition from postgraduate to early postdoctoral stages.",
