      ],
      "selection_notes": "Two qualified assessors independently review each eligible application and score it from 1 to 10 on four factors: quality of professional experience (30% weight), quality of professional recommendations (30% weight), quality of commitment to the applicant's home country (30% weight), and quality of education background (10% weight). The Secretariat uses the average of the two assessors plus a reasonably wide geographical distribution of awards, a reasonable distribution across gender, unusual circumstances or hardships, and giving scholarships to applicants who otherwise have limited access to financial resources for graduate studies abroad. Finalists go to the JJ/WBGSP Steering Committee, composed of members of the World Bank Board of Directors and World Bank management, for approval.",
      "notes": "Not eligible if admitted to a non-participating programme: the World Bank states explicitly that if you are not admitted to one of the participating master's programs you are not eligible to apply for a JJ/WBGSP scholarship in 2027. The scholarship does NOT cover visa application costs, expenses to bring and/or support family members, extra-curricular courses or training, courses below the minimum credits required for the degree, language training not provided by the graduate program, additional travel during the study program, research or supplementary educational materials, field trips, workshops, seminars or internships, educational equipment such as computers, and the resident permit fee. The scholarship may only supplement, and does not duplicate, any other source of financial support; if you hold another award you must send those documents to the JJ/WBGSP Secretariat. Scholars commit to return to their home country after earning their degree. Declining an earlier offer, or receiving the scholarship and failing to graduate, permanently bars reapplication. The 2026 window for Japanese nationals closed on Friday 17 April 2026, so only the 2027 developing-country-national cycle is live.",
      "detail": {
        "official_details": {
          "description": "The Joint Japan/World Bank Graduate Scholarship Program funds citizens of listed World Bank member developing countries who apply to master's programmes in development-related topics at one of 44 participating master's programmes across 24 universities in the U.S., Europe, Africa, Oceania and Japan. For 2027, subject to available funding, the scholarship pays tuition and basic medical insurance, a monthly subsistence allowance for living costs while on campus, economy class round-trip airfare between the scholar's home country and the host university, and a US$600 travel allowance per trip. The award covers the duration of the graduate programme or two years, whichever is less, and the scholar commits to return to their home country after graduating. Window #1 runs from 18 January to 26 February 2027, and the application form link is issued only to candidates who already hold an unconditional (except for funding) admission from a participating university and who have been shortlisted as eligible JJ/WBGSP applicants by that university.",
          "purpose": [
