      ],
      "requirements": [
        "Register interest with the JJ/WBGSP Secretariat by emailing scholarshipapplicants@worldbank.org from February 15, 2027",
        "Details on eligibility criteria for each call for applications are provided in that call's Application Guidelines, and those detailed criteria are strictly adhered to with no exceptions",
        "Be admitted unconditionally (except for financing) to, or currently enrolled full-time in, a graduate program outside of Japan in an area of study related to development, or be currently employed full-time in development-related work pertaining directly to developing countries",
        "Inform and document to the JJ/WBGSP Secretariat any other sources of scholarship funds if notified of being a semi-finalist or awarded a scholarship"
      ],
      "english_requirement": null,
      "application_method": [
        "From February 15, 2027, express interest to the JJ/WBGSP Secretariat by emailing scholarshipapplicants@worldbank.org",
        "Be admitted unconditionally (except for financing) to, or already be enrolled full-time in, a development-related master's or PhD programme outside Japan",
        "Gather the documents required by the 2027 Application Guidelines for Japanese Nationals",
        "Submit the application within the window closing April 16, 2027",
        "Await the JJ/WBGSC decision; winners are informed by mid-July"
      ],
      "selection_notes": "Two qualified assessors independently review each eligible application and score it on a scale of 1 to 10 on four factors: Quality of Academic Experience and Recommendation (20% weight), Quality of Professional Recommendations (20% weight), Quality of Professional Experience (20%), and Commitment to International Development Career (40% weight). The Secretariat uses the average score of the two assessors plus maintaining a reasonable distribution of awards across gender and giving scholarships to those applicants who, other things being equal, have limited access to financial resources for graduate studies abroad. Finalists are presented to the JJ/WBG Steering Committee, composed of members of the World Bank Board of Directors and World Bank management, for approval.",
      "notes": "The scholarship does NOT cover visa application costs, expenses to bring and/or support family members, extra-curricular courses or training, courses below the minimum credits required for the degree, language training not provided by the graduate program, additional travel during the study program, research or supplementary educational materials, field trips, workshops, seminars or internships, educational equipment such as computers, and the resident permit fee. The scholarship may only supplement, and does not duplicate, any other source of financial support; if you are notified of being a semi-finalist or are awarded, you must inform and document other scholarship funds to the Secretariat. After earning their degree, Japanese national scholars are expected to advance their professional career with a keen focus on the alleviation of poverty and enhanced shared prosperity in developing countries. The air travel benefit at the end of the scholarship period is conditional on having completed all requirements to earn the graduate degree, and the return ticket is to Japan or to a developing country where the scholar has secured employment. The published US$600 is a per-trip travel allowance, not the award total. The 2026 window for Japanese nationals closed on Friday 17 April 2026, so only the 2027 window is live.",
      "detail": {
        "official_details": {
          "description": "The Japanese nationals window of the Joint Japan/World Bank Graduate Scholarship Program awards scholarships of up to two years to a limited number of Japanese mid-career professionals who are committed to alleviating poverty and enhancing shared prosperity in developing countries and who are admitted to, or currently enrolled in, a master's or PhD programme outside Japan in a development-related field. The scholarship is fully funded by the Government of Japan and pays one economy class flight to the host university plus a US$600 travel allowance if the scholar is not already attending, one economy class flight back to Japan or a developing country where the scholar has secured employment plus a US$600 allowance on completion, tuition and basic medical insurance for the duration, and a monthly subsistence allowance while on campus. The 2027 window opens on 15 February and closes on 16 April 2027.",
          "purpose": [
            "The JJ/WBGSP provides scholarships for up to two-years to a limited number of Japanese mid-career professionals who are dedicated to the alleviation of poverty and enhanced shared prosperity in developing countries, and who have been admitted to or are currently enrolled in a master's or PhD graduate degree program outside of Japan and in development-related fields.",
            "After earning their degree, Japanese national scholars are expected to advance their professional career with a keen focus on the alleviation of poverty and enhanced shared prosperity in developing countries.",
            "The JJ/WBGSP uses a review process for applications from Japanese nationals aimed at identifying the candidates with the highest potential, after completion of their graduate studies, to impact international development.",
            "The scholarship is fully funded by the Government of Japan."
          ],
          "sdg_requirement": false,
