      "selection_notes": "Selection is merit-based and financial need is not a factor. Applicants must meet both the general and programme-specific entry requirements of the study programme and all eligibility criteria of the scholarship programme. The scholarship selection process runs at the same time as the programme admission selection process. Applicants who submit incomplete applications or apply late are not considered. Only about 2% of those who apply are granted a scholarship, and the scholarships available for application may vary from year to year. Applicants may apply for more than one scholarship but can only be awarded one Uppsala University scholarship.",
      "program_type": "Merit-based tuition fee scholarship for international fee-paying students",
      "best_fit": "Academically talented citizens of countries outside the EU/EEA and Switzerland who can cover their own living costs and who rank their chosen Uppsala University Master's programme first at University Admissions.",
      "notes": "The scholarship is funded by the Swedish Council for Higher Education, the government agency responsible for overseeing Swedish higher education. Students admitted to the Joint Nordic Master's Programme in Environmental Law, the Master's Programme in Biology NABiS, Master's Programme in Evolutionary Biology - MEME, Master's Programme in Implementation, Transformative Learning and Sustainability, Master's Programme in Religion in Peace and Conflict, Master's Programme in Transformative Game Design and Master's Programme in Turkic Studies are not eligible. The University also runs the Anders Wall Scholarship (full tuition fee for international Bachelor's or Master's students who are citizens of an African country, supported by The Beijer Foundation and Uppsala University). Uppsala runs a separate, much larger scholarship system for current students and researchers from affiliated foundations, which in 2025 distributed 79.5 million SEK across more than 500 scholarships.",
