      "last_verified_date": "2026-10-04",
      "region": "Europe",
      "description": "The OeAD, Austria's Agency for Education and Internationalisation, administers a large set of scholarship and grant programmes for students and researchers coming to Austria, financed mainly by the Austrian Federal Ministry for Women, Science and Research. Funding is not a single award: each programme has its own call, target group, duration, amount and deadline, and the calls are listed in the Austrian Database for Scholarships and Research Grants at www.grants.at. Incoming programmes include the Ernst Mach Grant for researchers worldwide, the Ernst Mach Grant for study at an Austrian University of Applied Sciences, the Ernst Mach Follow-up Grant, the Ernst Mach Grant - ASEA-UNINET, the Franz Werfel Fellowship and the Indonesia-Austria Scholarship Programme. Several calls for the 2027/2028 academic year are still open as of October 2026, with the earliest closing date on 1 December 2026.",
      "deadline": "Deadlines are set per programme. For the 2027/2028 academic year the OeAD lists: Ernst Mach Grant worldwide - 1 December 2026; Ernst Mach Grant for an Austrian University of Applied Sciences - 1 February 2027; Ernst Mach Follow-up Grant - 1 February 2027; Ernst Mach Grant ASEA-UNINET - 1 March 2027; Franz Werfel Fellowship - 1 March 2027 and 15 September 2027.",
      "deadline_date": null,
      "deadline_precision": "varies",
      "application_period": "Programme-specific. Calls for academic year 2027/2028 are open now, with deadlines from 1 December 2026 to 15 September 2027",
      "catalogue_url": "https://grants.at/",
      "official_updates_url": "https://oead.at/en/study-research-teaching/overview-grants-and-scholarships",
      "program_type": "Government-funded scholarship and grant programmes administered by a state-owned agency",
      "notes": "This row bundles many separate OeAD programmes rather than one award. Applicants must pick a specific call on grants.at. OeAD project support is described by the agency as funding \"primarily ... accommodation and travel expenses\", so full tuition funding must not be assumed. A separate BMFWF press release stated that the 2026/27 incoming programmes were worth EUR 15 million in total, which is an aggregate programme budget and not a per-scholarship amount.",
