      "name": "Ngarimu VC and 28th (Māori) Battalion Memorial Scholarships",
      "legacy_titles": [
        "Ngarimu VC and 28th (Māori) Battalion Memorial Scholarships 2026–2027"
      ],
      "country": "New Zealand",
      "region": "Oceania",
      "degree_levels": "Vocational (NZQA Levels 4–7), Undergraduate, Master's, Doctoral",
      "funding_type": "Partially Funded",
      "official_source": "Ngarimu VC and 28th (Māori) Battalion Memorial Scholarship Fund Board — Ministry of Education",
      "official_source_url": "https://www.education.govt.nz/parents-and-caregivers/tertiary/available-assistance/ngarimu-vc-and-28th-maori-battalion-memorial-scholarships",
      "status": "upcoming",
      "is_verified": true,
      "last_verified_date": "2026-10-04",
      "description": "The Ngarimu VC and 28th (Māori) Battalion Memorial Scholarships are annual awards funded by the Ngarimu VC and 28th (Māori) Battalion Memorial Scholarship Fund Board, established in 1945, and administered through the Ministry of Education. They are awarded to exceptional Māori students for vocational or tertiary study who demonstrate values aligned with those of the 28th (Māori) Battalion. Following a $100,000 per year increase from the 2026-27 Budget, 14 scholarships are awarded each year across five schemes: 5 VET, 4 undergraduate, 3 master's, 1 Master of Education research and 1 doctoral. Applicants must be of Māori descent or whakapapa to a recognised Māori iwi and be enrolling in or continuing full-time tertiary study.",
      "deadline": "Applications to support study in 2027 closed on Sunday 19 July 2026, and the Ministry expects to notify all applicants of outcomes by late November 2026. Applications to support study in 2028 will open mid-2027; no exact 2028 closing date is published.",
      "deadline_date": null,
      "deadline_precision": "recurring",
      "application_period": "Annual. 2027 study year: applications closed Sunday 19 July 2026, outcomes notified by late November 2026. 2028 study year: applications open mid-2027.",
      "eligibility_summary": "Applicants must be of Māori descent or whakapapa to a recognised Māori iwi, and must be enrolling in or already enrolled to continue full-time tertiary study for the relevant academic year. The selection panel gives preference to applicants whose studies aim to improve the social, economic and cultural wellbeing of Māori. VET applicants need a certificate or diploma at NZQA levels 4 to 7 (minimum 120 credits, full time) and cannot be studying a bachelor's degree.",
      "eligibility": [
        "Of Māori descent or whakapapa to a recognised Māori iwi",
        "Enrolling in, or already enrolled to continue, full-time tertiary study for the academic year the scholarship supports",
        "VET applicants: course leads to a certificate or diploma at NZQA levels 4 to 7, at least 120 credits total, studied full time",
        "VET applicants are not eligible if they are doing a bachelor's degree — they apply for the Ngarimu undergraduate scholarship instead",
        "VET level 4 apprenticeships are eligible if the applicant has completed years 1 to 2 and is enrolling in their third or fourth subsequent year",
        "Preference will be given to applicants whose studies aim to improve the social, economic and cultural wellbeing of Māori"
      ],
      "coverage": [
        "VET scholarships: $10,000 each for study of a minimum of 120 credits at NZQA level 4 to 7 non-degree",
        "Undergraduate scholarships: up to $30,000 each ($10,000 per year of full-time study for up to 3 years)",
        "Master's scholarships: up to $20,000 each ($10,000 per year of full-time study for up to 2 years)",
        "Master of Education research scholarship: up to $20,000 ($10,000 per year of full-time study for up to 2 years)",
        "Doctoral scholarship: up to $40,000 ($20,000 per year of full-time study for up to 2 years)"
      ],
      "required_documents": [
        "Record of achievement or other record of learning (verified tertiary academic transcript, MyBCITO record, NZQA record of achievement, or provider record) — for undergraduate applicants who have not yet started study, an NZQA record of learning for NCEA Level 2 (and Level 3 if applicable) or equivalent Cambridge International Examinations or International Baccalaureate",
        "Reference or endorsement commenting on academic achievement and suitability to study or training, from someone involved in the applicant's study or training journey — it cannot be from a friend or family member",
        "For master's and Master of Education research applicants: a brief statement of up to 250 words on the topic, reasons for interest in the subject area and future expectations",
        "For doctoral applicants: a 500-word essay on the thesis/doctorate topic and future expectations",
        "For doctoral applicants: a reference from the chief supervisor commenting on academic abilities and commitment, and confirming acceptance into the PhD programme"
